WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.988 align:middle line:90%
[SQUEAKING]

00:00:01.988 --> 00:00:03.973 align:middle line:90%
[RUSTLING]

00:00:03.973 --> 00:00:04.473 align:middle line:90%


00:00:04.473 --> 00:00:05.964 align:middle line:90%
[CLICKING]

00:00:05.964 --> 00:00:12.440 align:middle line:90%


00:00:12.440 --> 00:00:15.380 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: We are trying to
make our way through these four

00:00:15.380 --> 00:00:17.823 align:middle line:90%
as-yet intractable dilemmas.

00:00:17.823 --> 00:00:20.490 align:middle line:84%
The last time we met, we talked
about the curse of the familiar,

00:00:20.490 --> 00:00:22.307 align:middle line:90%
the ed tech Matthew effect.

00:00:22.307 --> 00:00:24.140 align:middle line:84%
Today, we're going to
be looking at the trap

00:00:24.140 --> 00:00:26.995 align:middle line:90%
of routine assessment.

00:00:26.995 --> 00:00:28.370 align:middle line:84%
Anybody want to
try to articulate

00:00:28.370 --> 00:00:33.110 align:middle line:84%
the core puzzle that's at
that's at the heart of the trap

00:00:33.110 --> 00:00:34.380 align:middle line:90%
of routine assessment?

00:00:34.380 --> 00:00:41.990 align:middle line:90%


00:00:41.990 --> 00:00:43.790 align:middle line:90%
What do you got, Dana?

00:00:43.790 --> 00:00:45.620 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Kind of
along the lines of,

00:00:45.620 --> 00:00:51.890 align:middle line:84%
teachers are teaching students
a subject, and schools have such

00:00:51.890 --> 00:00:55.820 align:middle line:84%
an emphasis on final
exams and what they're

00:00:55.820 --> 00:00:58.348 align:middle line:84%
preparing for these
standardized tests,

00:00:58.348 --> 00:00:59.890 align:middle line:84%
but we're also
fighting with the idea

00:00:59.890 --> 00:01:01.880 align:middle line:90%
of what a computer can grade.

00:01:01.880 --> 00:01:04.239 align:middle line:84%
So if you're trying to
standardize some sort of way

00:01:04.239 --> 00:01:06.100 align:middle line:84%
of grading something,
you can only

00:01:06.100 --> 00:01:08.140 align:middle line:84%
really focus on
teaching students things

00:01:08.140 --> 00:01:09.620 align:middle line:90%
that computers can do.

00:01:09.620 --> 00:01:12.520 align:middle line:84%
But if a computer can
already do it, then

00:01:12.520 --> 00:01:15.125 align:middle line:84%
why is that the emphasis of
what we're teaching students?

00:01:15.125 --> 00:01:17.750 align:middle line:84%
So it's kind of the idea that,
if you look at the Venn diagram,

00:01:17.750 --> 00:01:20.380 align:middle line:84%
there's this whole other
half of the subject

00:01:20.380 --> 00:01:24.490 align:middle line:84%
that ultimately we probably
should be focusing on.

00:01:24.490 --> 00:01:28.060 align:middle line:84%
But we are really only talking
about that overlapping aspect

00:01:28.060 --> 00:01:29.860 align:middle line:84%
of what students
we're saying should

00:01:29.860 --> 00:01:32.450 align:middle line:90%
learn because we can assess it.

00:01:32.450 --> 00:01:34.330 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: Good--
that the technologies

00:01:34.330 --> 00:01:36.563 align:middle line:84%
we have for assessment
constrain what

00:01:36.563 --> 00:01:38.480 align:middle line:84%
it is that we can assess
in any kind of scale.

00:01:38.480 --> 00:01:41.980 align:middle line:84%
Anybody want to add-- what
else seems important to Dana's

00:01:41.980 --> 00:01:43.290 align:middle line:90%
initial offering there?

00:01:43.290 --> 00:01:54.983 align:middle line:90%


00:01:54.983 --> 00:01:57.400 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I think something
you brought up in the reading,

00:01:57.400 --> 00:02:00.383 align:middle line:84%
as well, is that there's a
connection to the labor markets.

00:02:00.383 --> 00:02:03.050 align:middle line:84%
So schools are supposed to help
us prepare for the labor market,

00:02:03.050 --> 00:02:05.380 align:middle line:84%
but we're learning these routine
things that are being taken over

00:02:05.380 --> 00:02:05.963 align:middle line:90%
by automation.

00:02:05.963 --> 00:02:07.713 align:middle line:84%
How is that actually
doing what the school

00:02:07.713 --> 00:02:09.020 align:middle line:90%
is supposed to function for?

00:02:09.020 --> 00:02:09.978 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: OK, good.

00:02:09.978 --> 00:02:13.580 align:middle line:84%
So schools operate in between
a couple of different things.

00:02:13.580 --> 00:02:18.100 align:middle line:84%
One is, what do we need kids
to do changes over time,

00:02:18.100 --> 00:02:21.790 align:middle line:90%
as we develop new technologies.

00:02:21.790 --> 00:02:23.810 align:middle line:84%
None of you should teach
your students-- well,

00:02:23.810 --> 00:02:25.518 align:middle line:84%
I mean, you shouldn't
teach your students

00:02:25.518 --> 00:02:27.340 align:middle line:84%
to operate a loom
on the basis that

00:02:27.340 --> 00:02:29.480 align:middle line:84%
will be a source of gainful
employment for them,

00:02:29.480 --> 00:02:31.647 align:middle line:84%
although there probably are
a small number of people

00:02:31.647 --> 00:02:34.720 align:middle line:84%
who are very profitable
small business loom operators

00:02:34.720 --> 00:02:37.390 align:middle line:84%
and particular kinds of things,
but generally speaking, not

00:02:37.390 --> 00:02:39.430 align:middle line:90%
a good bet.

00:02:39.430 --> 00:02:42.320 align:middle line:84%
And so it makes sense that
as technologies come along,

00:02:42.320 --> 00:02:45.220 align:middle line:84%
we change the kinds of things
that we teach to young people.

00:02:45.220 --> 00:02:50.740 align:middle line:84%
And computers, in
various ways, once

00:02:50.740 --> 00:02:52.720 align:middle line:84%
a decade, over
the last 10 years,

00:02:52.720 --> 00:02:54.610 align:middle line:84%
cause new kinds of
problems for us.

00:02:54.610 --> 00:02:57.450 align:middle line:84%
As computers' capacities
expand, what we

00:02:57.450 --> 00:03:00.060 align:middle line:84%
think about teaching
people changes.

00:03:00.060 --> 00:03:03.657 align:middle line:84%
At the same time, as
computers, technologies expand,

00:03:03.657 --> 00:03:05.490 align:middle line:84%
there's maybe new or
different kinds of ways

00:03:05.490 --> 00:03:07.200 align:middle line:84%
that we can use
them for assessment.

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:10.920 align:middle line:84%
And so those two things are
interplaying with each other

00:03:10.920 --> 00:03:11.705 align:middle line:90%
all the time.

00:03:11.705 --> 00:03:14.370 align:middle line:90%


00:03:14.370 --> 00:03:15.580 align:middle line:90%
We have some seats up here.

00:03:15.580 --> 00:03:16.870 align:middle line:84%
There's plenty of
room could use.

00:03:16.870 --> 00:03:17.537 align:middle line:90%
Nice to see you.

00:03:17.537 --> 00:03:20.040 align:middle line:90%


00:03:20.040 --> 00:03:22.270 align:middle line:84%
Other introductory things
that seem important?

00:03:22.270 --> 00:03:22.770 align:middle line:90%
Omar?

00:03:22.770 --> 00:03:24.850 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Yeah, that's
not to say that--

00:03:24.850 --> 00:03:27.780 align:middle line:84%
and you mentioned
this in the reading--

00:03:27.780 --> 00:03:31.530 align:middle line:84%
we're not trying to say, don't
teach times tables and stuff

00:03:31.530 --> 00:03:33.840 align:middle line:84%
like that, where it's
like stuff that's--

00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:36.090 align:middle line:84%
yes, computers can
assess that, but stuff

00:03:36.090 --> 00:03:38.370 align:middle line:84%
that students need
fundamentally to build on

00:03:38.370 --> 00:03:40.880 align:middle line:84%
for other, more complex tasks
that they will learn down

00:03:40.880 --> 00:03:41.380 align:middle line:90%
the line.

00:03:41.380 --> 00:03:42.172 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Good.

00:03:42.172 --> 00:03:44.400 align:middle line:84%
That makes the whole
conversation even more

00:03:44.400 --> 00:03:47.130 align:middle line:84%
complicated because
there's stuff

00:03:47.130 --> 00:03:50.190 align:middle line:84%
that we need people to be
able to do in their lives.

00:03:50.190 --> 00:03:53.730 align:middle line:84%
There also might be a class of
things that's called "stuff that

00:03:53.730 --> 00:03:57.920 align:middle line:84%
you need to learn in order to be
able to do those complex things,

00:03:57.920 --> 00:04:00.640 align:middle line:84%
even though computers can
do those simpler things."

00:04:00.640 --> 00:04:03.670 align:middle line:84%
So there's some class
of things that computers

00:04:03.670 --> 00:04:06.323 align:middle line:84%
can do that we just don't need
humans to ever do anymore.

00:04:06.323 --> 00:04:08.740 align:middle line:84%
I think I joked with you all
that one of the first times I

00:04:08.740 --> 00:04:12.140 align:middle line:84%
talked with my students at MIT
about large language models,

00:04:12.140 --> 00:04:15.100 align:middle line:84%
that somebody said, I'm
using large language models

00:04:15.100 --> 00:04:17.865 align:middle line:84%
to do the LaTeX
formatting of my papers.

00:04:17.865 --> 00:04:18.740 align:middle line:90%
And it's like, great.

00:04:18.740 --> 00:04:20.120 align:middle line:90%
Never think about that again.

00:04:20.120 --> 00:04:21.760 align:middle line:84%
If no student ever
thinks about that

00:04:21.760 --> 00:04:23.177 align:middle line:84%
again for the rest
of their lives,

00:04:23.177 --> 00:04:25.720 align:middle line:84%
we're probably fine
as a civilization.

00:04:25.720 --> 00:04:29.450 align:middle line:84%
But single-digit multiplication
is not like that.

00:04:29.450 --> 00:04:32.457 align:middle line:84%
Single-digit multiplication can
be trivially done by computers,

00:04:32.457 --> 00:04:34.540 align:middle line:84%
but it's actually quite
important for human beings

00:04:34.540 --> 00:04:35.040 align:middle line:90%
to learn.

00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:39.718 align:middle line:84%
So there's some complexity
in those kinds of things.

00:04:39.718 --> 00:04:41.260 align:middle line:84%
Anything else that
people want to add

00:04:41.260 --> 00:04:47.578 align:middle line:84%
to just the initial framing of
the trap of routine assessment?

00:04:47.578 --> 00:04:49.370 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I guess just
to add on even more,

00:04:49.370 --> 00:04:51.650 align:middle line:84%
there is this aspect
that our society values

00:04:51.650 --> 00:04:54.860 align:middle line:84%
these routine assessments
so that, even though you're

00:04:54.860 --> 00:04:58.940 align:middle line:84%
trying to change this and branch
out and expand what we teach

00:04:58.940 --> 00:05:01.010 align:middle line:84%
students, they're still
always constrained

00:05:01.010 --> 00:05:02.540 align:middle line:90%
by the market itself.

00:05:02.540 --> 00:05:04.190 align:middle line:84%
And ultimately,
teachers have to fight

00:05:04.190 --> 00:05:06.480 align:middle line:84%
against that and their
own independent wishes.

00:05:06.480 --> 00:05:09.548 align:middle line:84%
So as much as we think you can
just do it, you can't actually.

00:05:09.548 --> 00:05:10.340 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Good.

00:05:10.340 --> 00:05:13.070 align:middle line:84%
So I mean, I think you could
go to almost any educator

00:05:13.070 --> 00:05:16.460 align:middle line:84%
and say something along
the lines of, like, are

00:05:16.460 --> 00:05:19.760 align:middle line:84%
standardized tests a good
mechanism for capturing

00:05:19.760 --> 00:05:22.140 align:middle line:84%
all of the important things
that students should know?

00:05:22.140 --> 00:05:23.788 align:middle line:90%
And the answer is, clearly not.

00:05:23.788 --> 00:05:25.580 align:middle line:84%
There's almost no
educator in the world who

00:05:25.580 --> 00:05:27.620 align:middle line:90%
believes that to be the case.

00:05:27.620 --> 00:05:29.870 align:middle line:84%
There's more debate
over the question of,

00:05:29.870 --> 00:05:34.070 align:middle line:84%
do standardized tests capture
some of the important things

00:05:34.070 --> 00:05:36.980 align:middle line:90%
that we want students to know?

00:05:36.980 --> 00:05:40.310 align:middle line:84%
And then a follow-on
problem that there's then

00:05:40.310 --> 00:05:42.860 align:middle line:84%
this class of stuff which is
pretty important for students

00:05:42.860 --> 00:05:45.530 align:middle line:84%
to know, but we're not
very good at assessing.

00:05:45.530 --> 00:05:48.770 align:middle line:84%
And it's hard to prioritize
those things in education

00:05:48.770 --> 00:05:52.053 align:middle line:84%
systems because it's hard to
reward students and reward

00:05:52.053 --> 00:05:54.220 align:middle line:84%
educators for teaching
things that we don't know how

00:05:54.220 --> 00:05:56.530 align:middle line:84%
to measure or don't
know how to measure

00:05:56.530 --> 00:05:58.340 align:middle line:90%
in some kind of systemic way.

00:05:58.340 --> 00:06:02.740 align:middle line:84%
We don't know how to measure
with the kind of fairness

00:06:02.740 --> 00:06:05.380 align:middle line:84%
that we would want to use
for high-stakes assessments

00:06:05.380 --> 00:06:08.342 align:middle line:84%
for things like graduation
exams or college entrance exams

00:06:08.342 --> 00:06:09.800 align:middle line:84%
or other kinds of
things like that.

00:06:09.800 --> 00:06:12.190 align:middle line:84%
So all of these are
parts of systems

00:06:12.190 --> 00:06:14.920 align:middle line:84%
that get wrestled with
every day by educators

00:06:14.920 --> 00:06:17.820 align:middle line:84%
at all different
kinds of levels.

00:06:17.820 --> 00:06:20.470 align:middle line:84%
In the United
States, teachers are

00:06:20.470 --> 00:06:23.962 align:middle line:84%
unusually responsible for
wrestling with these issues

00:06:23.962 --> 00:06:26.420 align:middle line:84%
because we don't have something
like a national curriculum.

00:06:26.420 --> 00:06:28.840 align:middle line:84%
We don't even really have
something like national entrance

00:06:28.840 --> 00:06:32.020 align:middle line:84%
exams or something
like that, where

00:06:32.020 --> 00:06:35.290 align:middle line:84%
the whole system has to
work together to respond

00:06:35.290 --> 00:06:36.290 align:middle line:90%
to some of these things.

00:06:36.290 --> 00:06:39.400 align:middle line:84%
Teachers in the United States
have unusual discretion

00:06:39.400 --> 00:06:42.430 align:middle line:84%
for thinking about these
kinds of challenges.

00:06:42.430 --> 00:06:44.140 align:middle line:84%
I thought the place
that I would start

00:06:44.140 --> 00:06:50.380 align:middle line:84%
with you all is a little bit
towards what Ty suggested.

00:06:50.380 --> 00:06:56.890 align:middle line:84%
And we're probably going back
20-ish years to one of the last

00:06:56.890 --> 00:07:02.350 align:middle line:84%
times computers really got
educators and labor economists

00:07:02.350 --> 00:07:03.413 align:middle line:90%
quite upset.

00:07:03.413 --> 00:07:05.080 align:middle line:84%
So we're in another
one of these moments

00:07:05.080 --> 00:07:09.065 align:middle line:84%
right now with LLMs where
people are pretty stressed out.

00:07:09.065 --> 00:07:10.940 align:middle line:84%
How is this going to
change the labor market?

00:07:10.940 --> 00:07:13.430 align:middle line:84%
How is this going to change
what we expect of our students?

00:07:13.430 --> 00:07:15.888 align:middle line:84%
How should this change school
systems and things like that?

00:07:15.888 --> 00:07:18.260 align:middle line:84%
And to me, it's always
helpful to remember, oh,

00:07:18.260 --> 00:07:19.810 align:middle line:90%
we've done this before.

00:07:19.810 --> 00:07:23.350 align:middle line:84%
The introduction of personal
computers into most households

00:07:23.350 --> 00:07:25.280 align:middle line:84%
in the United States,
that was a big deal.

00:07:25.280 --> 00:07:26.980 align:middle line:90%
It was a big change in the--

00:07:26.980 --> 00:07:29.870 align:middle line:84%
the introduction of personal
computers into workplaces,

00:07:29.870 --> 00:07:30.890 align:middle line:90%
that was a big deal.

00:07:30.890 --> 00:07:32.683 align:middle line:84%
The introduction
of the internet,

00:07:32.683 --> 00:07:34.600 align:middle line:84%
broadband internet
connection in these spaces,

00:07:34.600 --> 00:07:37.450 align:middle line:84%
that was a big deal that
evoked all of these questions

00:07:37.450 --> 00:07:38.950 align:middle line:90%
in the past.

00:07:38.950 --> 00:07:43.780 align:middle line:84%
And one of the best summary
pieces to read from probably--

00:07:43.780 --> 00:07:49.000 align:middle line:84%
what was the 2015 summary from
this debate is a paper called

00:07:49.000 --> 00:07:52.330 align:middle line:84%
"Dancing With Robots" by two
economists, one who is here,

00:07:52.330 --> 00:07:54.880 align:middle line:84%
Frank Levy, and another
who is at Harvard,

00:07:54.880 --> 00:07:57.270 align:middle line:84%
who was actually my
advisor, Richard Murnane.

00:07:57.270 --> 00:07:59.550 align:middle line:84%
This was based on another
book they wrote called

00:07:59.550 --> 00:08:01.680 align:middle line:90%
The New Division of Labor.

00:08:01.680 --> 00:08:04.380 align:middle line:84%
And the original research
for The New Division of Labor

00:08:04.380 --> 00:08:07.320 align:middle line:84%
is kind of right when
personal computers are really

00:08:07.320 --> 00:08:09.630 align:middle line:84%
starting to widely spread
in workplaces, in people's

00:08:09.630 --> 00:08:11.460 align:middle line:90%
homes, and things like that.

00:08:11.460 --> 00:08:15.882 align:middle line:84%
An early bet that economists
made was something like--

00:08:15.882 --> 00:08:18.090 align:middle line:84%
one of the things economists
think about all the time

00:08:18.090 --> 00:08:19.960 align:middle line:90%
is, what do jobs require?

00:08:19.960 --> 00:08:22.890 align:middle line:84%
And education economists then
start thinking about, OK,

00:08:22.890 --> 00:08:24.390 align:middle line:84%
what do jobs require,
and how do you

00:08:24.390 --> 00:08:26.850 align:middle line:84%
teach people the things that
the jobs that we actually have

00:08:26.850 --> 00:08:28.650 align:middle line:90%
require?--

00:08:28.650 --> 00:08:32.549 align:middle line:84%
that computers are going to
complement high-wage workers,

00:08:32.549 --> 00:08:35.110 align:middle line:84%
and they're going to
replace low-wage workers.

00:08:35.110 --> 00:08:39.240 align:middle line:84%
That was a pretty early bet that
a bunch of economists had had.

00:08:39.240 --> 00:08:41.857 align:middle line:84%
And there was something
about that to Frank Levy

00:08:41.857 --> 00:08:43.690 align:middle line:84%
and Richard Murnane
that didn't smell right.

00:08:43.690 --> 00:08:45.570 align:middle line:84%
They were like, I
think it's going

00:08:45.570 --> 00:08:49.590 align:middle line:84%
to end up being more
complicated than that.

00:08:49.590 --> 00:08:54.240 align:middle line:84%
So they thought a lot about
the specific kinds of things

00:08:54.240 --> 00:08:58.260 align:middle line:84%
that people did in jobs and
how those jobs were changing.

00:08:58.260 --> 00:09:02.670 align:middle line:84%
How many of you have ever
checked in to get on a flight

00:09:02.670 --> 00:09:05.780 align:middle line:84%
by talking directly
to a human being?

00:09:05.780 --> 00:09:07.710 align:middle line:90%
OK, so it looks like people--

00:09:07.710 --> 00:09:11.050 align:middle line:84%
how many of you, the majority
of times you get on a flight,

00:09:11.050 --> 00:09:12.810 align:middle line:90%
you check in with a machine?

00:09:12.810 --> 00:09:14.843 align:middle line:84%
OK, so this is
actually surprising.

00:09:14.843 --> 00:09:16.260 align:middle line:84%
I'm surprised that
almost everyone

00:09:16.260 --> 00:09:18.690 align:middle line:84%
in the room who raised their
hand said that at least once.

00:09:18.690 --> 00:09:22.230 align:middle line:84%
When I first put together
this slide, like 15 years ago

00:09:22.230 --> 00:09:24.940 align:middle line:84%
or something like that, this
was a pretty novel experience.

00:09:24.940 --> 00:09:28.170 align:middle line:84%
So when I was in
college, you would not

00:09:28.170 --> 00:09:30.660 align:middle line:84%
interact with a machine at
all to get onto a flight.

00:09:30.660 --> 00:09:32.130 align:middle line:84%
You would have
called the airline

00:09:32.130 --> 00:09:34.420 align:middle line:84%
or called the travel
agent to buy a ticket.

00:09:34.420 --> 00:09:35.860 align:middle line:84%
You would have a
physical ticket.

00:09:35.860 --> 00:09:36.880 align:middle line:84%
You would take that
physical ticket.

00:09:36.880 --> 00:09:38.543 align:middle line:84%
You would show it
to a human being.

00:09:38.543 --> 00:09:40.710 align:middle line:84%
Now, the interesting thing
about those conversations

00:09:40.710 --> 00:09:43.540 align:middle line:84%
with human beings was that
they were highly standardized.

00:09:43.540 --> 00:09:44.680 align:middle line:90%
They were highly routine.

00:09:44.680 --> 00:09:48.870 align:middle line:84%
So post 9/11 it would
be, who are you?

00:09:48.870 --> 00:09:49.580 align:middle line:90%
This is my ID.

00:09:49.580 --> 00:09:50.730 align:middle line:90%
Where are you going?

00:09:50.730 --> 00:09:51.480 align:middle line:90%
This is my ticket.

00:09:51.480 --> 00:09:52.170 align:middle line:90%
Do you have a ticket?

00:09:52.170 --> 00:09:52.890 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, this is my ticket.

00:09:52.890 --> 00:09:53.723 align:middle line:90%
Where are you going?

00:09:53.723 --> 00:09:54.860 align:middle line:90%
This is where I'm going.

00:09:54.860 --> 00:09:55.730 align:middle line:90%
Do you have any bags with you?

00:09:55.730 --> 00:09:56.120 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

00:09:56.120 --> 00:09:57.510 align:middle line:90%
Did you pack your bags yourself?

00:09:57.510 --> 00:09:58.970 align:middle line:84%
Have they been in your
possession the whole time?

00:09:58.970 --> 00:09:59.720 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

00:09:59.720 --> 00:10:01.020 align:middle line:90%
And then off you go.

00:10:01.020 --> 00:10:06.770 align:middle line:84%
So the vast majority of
times that a person went

00:10:06.770 --> 00:10:09.470 align:middle line:84%
to a ticketing agent to have
a conversation with them,

00:10:09.470 --> 00:10:12.038 align:middle line:84%
highly structured,
highly routined.

00:10:12.038 --> 00:10:13.580 align:middle line:84%
We know what kinds
of questions we're

00:10:13.580 --> 00:10:14.820 align:middle line:90%
going to get as passengers.

00:10:14.820 --> 00:10:16.320 align:middle line:84%
We know what kind
of inputs we need.

00:10:16.320 --> 00:10:17.910 align:middle line:84%
We have the conversation
successfully.

00:10:17.910 --> 00:10:19.040 align:middle line:90%
We move on.

00:10:19.040 --> 00:10:22.800 align:middle line:84%
And so as computing technology
becomes more sophisticated,

00:10:22.800 --> 00:10:24.675 align:middle line:84%
as user interface becomes
more sophisticated,

00:10:24.675 --> 00:10:26.425 align:middle line:84%
as all these things
become less expensive,

00:10:26.425 --> 00:10:29.000 align:middle line:84%
someone's like, hey, we can
just program those conversations

00:10:29.000 --> 00:10:30.560 align:middle line:90%
into a machine.

00:10:30.560 --> 00:10:32.690 align:middle line:84%
And so now,
overwhelmingly, you have

00:10:32.690 --> 00:10:34.248 align:middle line:90%
that exact same conversation.

00:10:34.248 --> 00:10:36.540 align:middle line:84%
You just push the buttons to
say, yes, I have a ticket.

00:10:36.540 --> 00:10:37.230 align:middle line:90%
Yes, this is me.

00:10:37.230 --> 00:10:39.680 align:middle line:84%
Yes, my bags have been in my
possession the whole time.

00:10:39.680 --> 00:10:44.160 align:middle line:84%
However, for as long
as we've had kiosks,

00:10:44.160 --> 00:10:47.060 align:middle line:84%
there has always been
a number of cases

00:10:47.060 --> 00:10:49.910 align:middle line:84%
in which the kiosks
can't respond

00:10:49.910 --> 00:10:52.250 align:middle line:90%
to the demands of the moment.

00:10:52.250 --> 00:10:54.870 align:middle line:84%
So there's something really
weird about your ticket.

00:10:54.870 --> 00:10:57.230 align:middle line:84%
There's some problem that's
gone on with your booking

00:10:57.230 --> 00:11:00.560 align:middle line:84%
that the machine hasn't
anticipated or can't understand,

00:11:00.560 --> 00:11:03.950 align:middle line:84%
or you have some kind
of communication issue.

00:11:03.950 --> 00:11:06.260 align:middle line:84%
For some reason, a
human being can't

00:11:06.260 --> 00:11:07.440 align:middle line:90%
communicate with the kiosk.

00:11:07.440 --> 00:11:09.210 align:middle line:84%
Maybe their glasses
are broken that day.

00:11:09.210 --> 00:11:11.690 align:middle line:84%
Maybe they are
trying to travel--

00:11:11.690 --> 00:11:14.090 align:middle line:84%
they don't speak the language
that the kiosk is in.

00:11:14.090 --> 00:11:17.340 align:middle line:84%
They're so angry at the airline
for what they've messed up,

00:11:17.340 --> 00:11:19.380 align:middle line:84%
they just go banging
on the kiosk.

00:11:19.380 --> 00:11:20.630 align:middle line:90%
So you've got to think a lot--

00:11:20.630 --> 00:11:22.340 align:middle line:84%
there's one person
behind this row

00:11:22.340 --> 00:11:27.350 align:middle line:84%
of kiosks whose job it is to
have all the conversations

00:11:27.350 --> 00:11:29.510 align:middle line:90%
that the kiosk can't have.

00:11:29.510 --> 00:11:31.240 align:middle line:84%
If you're a labor
economist, that's

00:11:31.240 --> 00:11:32.490 align:middle line:90%
a pretty interesting question.

00:11:32.490 --> 00:11:36.260 align:middle line:84%
What is that human being
doing that we couldn't program

00:11:36.260 --> 00:11:37.695 align:middle line:90%
into all of those machines?

00:11:37.695 --> 00:11:40.070 align:middle line:84%
That was the kind of question
that Frank Levy and Richard

00:11:40.070 --> 00:11:41.360 align:middle line:90%
Murnane were kind of asking.

00:11:41.360 --> 00:11:43.550 align:middle line:84%
In a world where
there's lots of work

00:11:43.550 --> 00:11:45.460 align:middle line:84%
that we can put on
computers, what is it

00:11:45.460 --> 00:11:47.050 align:middle line:90%
that humans are still good at?

00:11:47.050 --> 00:11:49.180 align:middle line:84%
In economists terms, what
is it that humans still

00:11:49.180 --> 00:11:53.140 align:middle line:84%
have a comparative advantage
over computers around?

00:11:53.140 --> 00:11:55.603 align:middle line:84%
Here is another
funny example, which

00:11:55.603 --> 00:11:57.020 align:middle line:84%
I think I wrote
about in the book,

00:11:57.020 --> 00:11:58.930 align:middle line:84%
about how some of
these things change.

00:11:58.930 --> 00:12:02.680 align:middle line:84%
So in something like
1987, my parents

00:12:02.680 --> 00:12:07.270 align:middle line:84%
bought a cabin in Vermont
which had an old boiler in it,

00:12:07.270 --> 00:12:09.940 align:middle line:84%
like a hot water heater
that was attached to a tank.

00:12:09.940 --> 00:12:12.230 align:middle line:90%
And my mom passed away.

00:12:12.230 --> 00:12:13.250 align:middle line:90%
I inherited the house.

00:12:13.250 --> 00:12:17.200 align:middle line:84%
So sometime around 2007, about
20 years later, this boiler

00:12:17.200 --> 00:12:18.970 align:middle line:84%
dies, and I'm
responsible for it.

00:12:18.970 --> 00:12:21.370 align:middle line:84%
The original boiler
was installed

00:12:21.370 --> 00:12:24.550 align:middle line:84%
by this guy named Dan Masterson,
who lived on Masterson Lane

00:12:24.550 --> 00:12:26.110 align:middle line:90%
in Bethel, Vermont.

00:12:26.110 --> 00:12:27.730 align:middle line:84%
And I was trying
to find a plumber

00:12:27.730 --> 00:12:29.480 align:middle line:84%
to replace this
hot water heater.

00:12:29.480 --> 00:12:31.160 align:middle line:84%
And the guy I found
was Dan Masterson,

00:12:31.160 --> 00:12:33.560 align:middle line:84%
who lived on Masterson
Lane in Bethel, Vermont.

00:12:33.560 --> 00:12:35.560 align:middle line:84%
So the guy had been a
plumber for 20 years,

00:12:35.560 --> 00:12:38.620 align:middle line:84%
and we decided that instead of
doing an old-school hot water

00:12:38.620 --> 00:12:41.930 align:middle line:84%
boiler, we're going to install
one of these on-demand systems.

00:12:41.930 --> 00:12:44.250 align:middle line:84%
So this is a very
beautiful picture to me

00:12:44.250 --> 00:12:46.560 align:middle line:84%
because there's no hot
water tank that can freeze.

00:12:46.560 --> 00:12:48.640 align:middle line:84%
It just like heats up the
water exactly when you need it.

00:12:48.640 --> 00:12:50.410 align:middle line:84%
You can see how nicely
all these pipes are.

00:12:50.410 --> 00:12:52.368 align:middle line:84%
The last building had
pipes all over the place,

00:12:52.368 --> 00:12:53.460 align:middle line:90%
things like that.

00:12:53.460 --> 00:12:55.420 align:middle line:90%
So Dan installs this system.

00:12:55.420 --> 00:12:57.840 align:middle line:84%
Dan, as you can imagine now,
is getting towards the end

00:12:57.840 --> 00:13:00.360 align:middle line:90%
of his career as a plumber.

00:13:00.360 --> 00:13:01.260 align:middle line:90%
And I say, great.

00:13:01.260 --> 00:13:03.083 align:middle line:84%
Dan, show me how
to use this system.

00:13:03.083 --> 00:13:04.500 align:middle line:84%
He's like, well,
you walk in here,

00:13:04.500 --> 00:13:06.210 align:middle line:90%
and you open this little door.

00:13:06.210 --> 00:13:08.790 align:middle line:84%
And when you look at the
system, it should say, "Good."

00:13:08.790 --> 00:13:10.585 align:middle line:90%
[LAUGHTER]

00:13:10.585 --> 00:13:13.920 align:middle line:84%
I was like, all right, Dan,
what do I do if it doesn't say

00:13:13.920 --> 00:13:14.500 align:middle line:90%
"Good."

00:13:14.500 --> 00:13:15.570 align:middle line:90%
You guys can't see this well.

00:13:15.570 --> 00:13:17.487 align:middle line:84%
He's like, see this
little button that says R?

00:13:17.487 --> 00:13:18.900 align:middle line:90%
Push that.

00:13:18.900 --> 00:13:20.380 align:middle line:90%
So that's the Reset button.

00:13:20.380 --> 00:13:22.505 align:middle line:84%
I was like, all right, I
can push the Reset button.

00:13:22.505 --> 00:13:24.537 align:middle line:84%
Well, what do I do
if that doesn't work?

00:13:24.537 --> 00:13:26.620 align:middle line:84%
He's like, you see this
electrical outlet up here?

00:13:26.620 --> 00:13:29.410 align:middle line:84%
Just unplug it, and
plug it back in.

00:13:29.410 --> 00:13:31.560 align:middle line:84%
I was like, all right,
I will recycle the power

00:13:31.560 --> 00:13:33.970 align:middle line:84%
in my hot water heater
if that doesn't work.

00:13:33.970 --> 00:13:37.050 align:middle line:84%
And I was like, OK, so it
doesn't say, "Good," I've hit R,

00:13:37.050 --> 00:13:38.800 align:middle line:84%
I've unplugged it,
it's still not working.

00:13:38.800 --> 00:13:39.550 align:middle line:90%
Then what do I do?

00:13:39.550 --> 00:13:42.690 align:middle line:84%
He says, there's one guy in the
upper River Valley of Vermont

00:13:42.690 --> 00:13:46.260 align:middle line:84%
who has flown to Scandinavia
to get the training on how

00:13:46.260 --> 00:13:47.650 align:middle line:90%
to reprogram these systems.

00:13:47.650 --> 00:13:51.000 align:middle line:84%
And so if those two things
don't work, go find that guy.

00:13:51.000 --> 00:13:53.490 align:middle line:90%
And I tried to imagine--

00:13:53.490 --> 00:13:57.690 align:middle line:84%
Dan Masterson, probably
in 1970, 1975 or something

00:13:57.690 --> 00:14:00.060 align:middle line:84%
like that, going through
his plumbing apprenticeship

00:14:00.060 --> 00:14:01.260 align:middle line:90%
training.

00:14:01.260 --> 00:14:03.953 align:middle line:84%
And he learned how to
solder pipes together

00:14:03.953 --> 00:14:06.120 align:middle line:84%
and how to do some basic
electrical stuff and things

00:14:06.120 --> 00:14:07.540 align:middle line:90%
like that.

00:14:07.540 --> 00:14:09.790 align:middle line:84%
If he went to the
same training now,

00:14:09.790 --> 00:14:12.390 align:middle line:84%
you have to be an apprentice
computer programmer

00:14:12.390 --> 00:14:13.300 align:middle line:90%
to be a plumber.

00:14:13.300 --> 00:14:16.890 align:middle line:84%
And not only that,
the systems are

00:14:16.890 --> 00:14:20.220 align:middle line:84%
designed to solve all
of the easy problems.

00:14:20.220 --> 00:14:23.383 align:middle line:84%
If a problem is easy, it'll
just solve it itself on reboot.

00:14:23.383 --> 00:14:25.800 align:middle line:84%
The things that you have to
be able to solve as a computer

00:14:25.800 --> 00:14:29.208 align:middle line:84%
programming plumber are the
ill-structured problems,

00:14:29.208 --> 00:14:30.750 align:middle line:84%
like when the system
can't figure out

00:14:30.750 --> 00:14:33.070 align:middle line:84%
what's wrong with itself
and can't correct itself

00:14:33.070 --> 00:14:34.240 align:middle line:90%
and those kinds of things.

00:14:34.240 --> 00:14:36.090 align:middle line:84%
So, I mean, the
reason I'm trying

00:14:36.090 --> 00:14:38.400 align:middle line:84%
to bring these two
cases to your attention

00:14:38.400 --> 00:14:40.470 align:middle line:84%
is we could say,
all right, there

00:14:40.470 --> 00:14:43.770 align:middle line:84%
are some classes of jobs that
are going to disappear and be

00:14:43.770 --> 00:14:44.950 align:middle line:90%
replaced by computers.

00:14:44.950 --> 00:14:46.230 align:middle line:90%
There used to be--

00:14:46.230 --> 00:14:47.440 align:middle line:84%
I mean, the other thing
about these people,

00:14:47.440 --> 00:14:49.940 align:middle line:84%
if you're just thinking about
this in a broader social con--

00:14:49.940 --> 00:14:52.440 align:middle line:84%
there used to be six
people behind this desk.

00:14:52.440 --> 00:14:53.800 align:middle line:90%
And they wore uniforms.

00:14:53.800 --> 00:14:54.730 align:middle line:90%
Maybe they had a college degree.

00:14:54.730 --> 00:14:56.272 align:middle line:84%
Maybe they had an
associate's degree.

00:14:56.272 --> 00:14:58.118 align:middle line:84%
They probably made a
middle-class living.

00:14:58.118 --> 00:15:00.660 align:middle line:84%
You could make a decent living
as a person who checked people

00:15:00.660 --> 00:15:01.570 align:middle line:90%
in to airlines.

00:15:01.570 --> 00:15:03.810 align:middle line:90%
And most of those jobs are gone.

00:15:03.810 --> 00:15:06.490 align:middle line:84%
And even in places
where jobs remained,

00:15:06.490 --> 00:15:10.770 align:middle line:84%
they're being transformed
by the capacities computer

00:15:10.770 --> 00:15:13.140 align:middle line:90%
offer in these systems.

00:15:13.140 --> 00:15:15.300 align:middle line:84%
So one of the cool
things about the research

00:15:15.300 --> 00:15:20.370 align:middle line:84%
in The New Division of Labor
is that they basically go--

00:15:20.370 --> 00:15:24.900 align:middle line:84%
the Department of Labor collects
all of these names of jobs

00:15:24.900 --> 00:15:26.850 align:middle line:90%
and the description of jobs.

00:15:26.850 --> 00:15:29.250 align:middle line:84%
And they do an analysis
of those descriptions

00:15:29.250 --> 00:15:31.810 align:middle line:84%
to see how they're
changing over time.

00:15:31.810 --> 00:15:37.590 align:middle line:84%
And what they find is that
you can break jobs down

00:15:37.590 --> 00:15:40.780 align:middle line:90%
into maybe four categories.

00:15:40.780 --> 00:15:43.640 align:middle line:84%
One of those categories would
be routine manual jobs--

00:15:43.640 --> 00:15:46.340 align:middle line:84%
so that involves jobs that are
lifting, moving things around,

00:15:46.340 --> 00:15:49.360 align:middle line:84%
stuff like that--
routine cognitive work--

00:15:49.360 --> 00:15:52.460 align:middle line:84%
so that would be things like
filing and stuff like that.

00:15:52.460 --> 00:15:55.480 align:middle line:84%
That would be things like having
standardized conversations

00:15:55.480 --> 00:15:59.470 align:middle line:90%
to check people into an airline.

00:15:59.470 --> 00:16:03.290 align:middle line:84%
Two others are what they call
ill-structured problem solving--

00:16:03.290 --> 00:16:05.320 align:middle line:84%
so solving any kind
of problem where

00:16:05.320 --> 00:16:06.905 align:middle line:84%
you don't know what
inputs you need,

00:16:06.905 --> 00:16:09.280 align:middle line:84%
you don't know what the outputs
look like, you don't know

00:16:09.280 --> 00:16:10.700 align:middle line:90%
what the correct answer is.

00:16:10.700 --> 00:16:13.480 align:middle line:84%
You're solving that as you're
figuring out the problem--

00:16:13.480 --> 00:16:18.790 align:middle line:84%
or complex communication, tasks
that require figuring out--

00:16:18.790 --> 00:16:21.950 align:middle line:84%
any kind of solving the problem
requires talking to people.

00:16:21.950 --> 00:16:24.690 align:middle line:84%
Some of that could be marketing
and sales kind of thing,

00:16:24.690 --> 00:16:25.940 align:middle line:90%
figuring out what people want.

00:16:25.940 --> 00:16:27.857 align:middle line:84%
But other than that, it
could be a home health

00:16:27.857 --> 00:16:31.010 align:middle line:84%
aide does a ton of
complex communication.

00:16:31.010 --> 00:16:35.260 align:middle line:84%
And their basic thesis was
that things that are routine

00:16:35.260 --> 00:16:38.540 align:middle line:84%
are disappearing from the
American labor market.

00:16:38.540 --> 00:16:42.740 align:middle line:84%
This is a huge problem if you
want to have a stable society.

00:16:42.740 --> 00:16:44.700 align:middle line:84%
In the 20th century,
there were many,

00:16:44.700 --> 00:16:48.530 align:middle line:84%
many people who performed
routine labor for a reasonable,

00:16:48.530 --> 00:16:52.010 align:middle line:84%
middle-class wage and had a
perfectly suitable life doing

00:16:52.010 --> 00:16:53.060 align:middle line:90%
it.

00:16:53.060 --> 00:16:55.220 align:middle line:84%
Some of the kinds of
jobs that are growing

00:16:55.220 --> 00:16:59.505 align:middle line:84%
are service jobs that
don't really require--

00:16:59.505 --> 00:17:01.880 align:middle line:84%
they're a little bit of routine
manual work, a little bit

00:17:01.880 --> 00:17:03.650 align:middle line:84%
of routine cognitive
work, and they

00:17:03.650 --> 00:17:07.730 align:middle line:84%
pay the absolute
minimum wage possible.

00:17:07.730 --> 00:17:09.619 align:middle line:84%
That's a little
bit less concerning

00:17:09.619 --> 00:17:15.140 align:middle line:84%
today where you can get jobs
doing service work that pay--

00:17:15.140 --> 00:17:17.720 align:middle line:84%
you can work at some place in
McDonald's and make $15 an hour

00:17:17.720 --> 00:17:19.760 align:middle line:84%
or $17 an hour,
things like that.

00:17:19.760 --> 00:17:22.890 align:middle line:84%
But when there was a surplus in
the labor market 10 years ago,

00:17:22.890 --> 00:17:24.619 align:middle line:84%
12 years ago, all
those people would

00:17:24.619 --> 00:17:27.500 align:middle line:84%
have been paid $7.25 or whatever
the federal minimum wage was

00:17:27.500 --> 00:17:28.610 align:middle line:90%
at the time.

00:17:28.610 --> 00:17:30.710 align:middle line:84%
But the two kinds of
jobs that were growing

00:17:30.710 --> 00:17:33.920 align:middle line:84%
were things that required
non-routine analytical skills

00:17:33.920 --> 00:17:34.760 align:middle line:90%
and social skills.

00:17:34.760 --> 00:17:36.510 align:middle line:84%
Some of this was updated
by this guy David

00:17:36.510 --> 00:17:40.350 align:middle line:84%
Autor who's an economist
who's here now, as well.

00:17:40.350 --> 00:17:44.970 align:middle line:84%
So if you're an educator, the
thing you got to be thinking

00:17:44.970 --> 00:17:50.170 align:middle line:84%
is, like, man, we have a
different job in front of us.

00:17:50.170 --> 00:17:52.170 align:middle line:84%
It's not sufficient
to prepare people

00:17:52.170 --> 00:17:56.743 align:middle line:84%
to do jobs that require routine
cognitive work or routine manual

00:17:56.743 --> 00:17:58.410 align:middle line:84%
work because those
jobs are disappearing

00:17:58.410 --> 00:17:59.710 align:middle line:90%
as a part of the labor market.

00:17:59.710 --> 00:18:01.980 align:middle line:84%
And even when the
same job title exists,

00:18:01.980 --> 00:18:04.110 align:middle line:84%
like they're still
plumbers, but plumbers

00:18:04.110 --> 00:18:06.480 align:middle line:84%
are increasingly
called upon to do

00:18:06.480 --> 00:18:11.250 align:middle line:84%
ill-structured problem solving
and to do complex communication.

00:18:11.250 --> 00:18:13.720 align:middle line:84%
This was an update of
this, I guess, from 2012.

00:18:13.720 --> 00:18:15.486 align:middle line:90%
This is just another--

00:18:15.486 --> 00:18:18.850 align:middle line:84%
here's a 2-by-2, where jobs that
require social skills are here.

00:18:18.850 --> 00:18:22.140 align:middle line:84%
Jobs that require
math skills are here.

00:18:22.140 --> 00:18:24.760 align:middle line:84%
The jobs that are in green
are the ones that are growing.

00:18:24.760 --> 00:18:27.670 align:middle line:84%
The jobs that are in red are
the ones that are disappearing.

00:18:27.670 --> 00:18:29.760 align:middle line:84%
And you can see that
they're clustered

00:18:29.760 --> 00:18:33.090 align:middle line:84%
in the things that require
social skills are growing,

00:18:33.090 --> 00:18:35.790 align:middle line:84%
and the things that require
fewer social skills are

00:18:35.790 --> 00:18:37.170 align:middle line:90%
disappearing.

00:18:37.170 --> 00:18:41.280 align:middle line:84%
Truck drivers is one
that people are often,

00:18:41.280 --> 00:18:43.300 align:middle line:84%
particularly at a time
when people were pretty--

00:18:43.300 --> 00:18:45.280 align:middle line:90%
this was 2015, 2017.

00:18:45.280 --> 00:18:47.860 align:middle line:84%
There are 3.5 million truck
drivers in the United States.

00:18:47.860 --> 00:18:49.652 align:middle line:84%
That's about one in
every hundred Americans

00:18:49.652 --> 00:18:50.970 align:middle line:90%
is a truck driver.

00:18:50.970 --> 00:18:57.750 align:middle line:84%
And probably 10 years ago was
some of the headiest time where

00:18:57.750 --> 00:19:00.630 align:middle line:84%
computer scientists believed
that it was reasonably

00:19:00.630 --> 00:19:02.875 align:middle line:84%
possible to replace
all of those people,

00:19:02.875 --> 00:19:05.250 align:middle line:84%
that you would be able to
develop artificial intelligence

00:19:05.250 --> 00:19:07.150 align:middle line:84%
systems that would be
able to drive trucks.

00:19:07.150 --> 00:19:11.030 align:middle line:84%
So Uber went to Carnegie Mellon
and bought their whole robotics

00:19:11.030 --> 00:19:11.530 align:middle line:90%
department.

00:19:11.530 --> 00:19:13.738 align:middle line:84%
They didn't actually pay
for the robotics department.

00:19:13.738 --> 00:19:15.600 align:middle line:84%
You just pay each
individual faculty member

00:19:15.600 --> 00:19:18.420 align:middle line:84%
to go leave and
join your company.

00:19:18.420 --> 00:19:22.297 align:middle line:84%
And there's still 3.7 million
truck drivers in the United

00:19:22.297 --> 00:19:24.630 align:middle line:84%
States or however many there
are because this turned out

00:19:24.630 --> 00:19:27.300 align:middle line:84%
to be a much harder
problem than we thought.

00:19:27.300 --> 00:19:29.580 align:middle line:84%
But this is the
kind of angst that

00:19:29.580 --> 00:19:33.110 align:middle line:84%
exists in society when these
new systems are developed.

00:19:33.110 --> 00:19:36.570 align:middle line:90%
What would it be like if--

00:19:36.570 --> 00:19:37.970 align:middle line:84%
what if every kid
in school today

00:19:37.970 --> 00:19:39.620 align:middle line:84%
who's destined to become a
truck driver, we have to find

00:19:39.620 --> 00:19:40.870 align:middle line:90%
something else for them to do?

00:19:40.870 --> 00:19:44.390 align:middle line:84%
That's a pretty challenging
thing to think about.

00:19:44.390 --> 00:19:47.130 align:middle line:90%
This was another 2017 example.

00:19:47.130 --> 00:19:48.030 align:middle line:90%
And I don't know.

00:19:48.030 --> 00:19:50.840 align:middle line:84%
I was glad to go
back and revisit this

00:19:50.840 --> 00:19:53.750 align:middle line:84%
because there's lots of
things that are going on today

00:19:53.750 --> 00:19:56.240 align:middle line:84%
about, like, man, LLMs are
going to replace all this work

00:19:56.240 --> 00:19:57.157 align:middle line:90%
that people are doing.

00:19:57.157 --> 00:19:58.790 align:middle line:84%
But for the last
two decades, we've

00:19:58.790 --> 00:20:00.380 align:middle line:84%
been building
software that replaces

00:20:00.380 --> 00:20:01.500 align:middle line:90%
the work that people are doing.

00:20:01.500 --> 00:20:02.780 align:middle line:84%
There's all kinds
of machine learning

00:20:02.780 --> 00:20:05.130 align:middle line:84%
based-systems that can do what
lawyers do and things like that.

00:20:05.130 --> 00:20:06.990 align:middle line:84%
You don't necessarily
need LLMs to do it,

00:20:06.990 --> 00:20:09.260 align:middle line:84%
although maybe it
will accelerate

00:20:09.260 --> 00:20:12.260 align:middle line:90%
some of these kinds of things.

00:20:12.260 --> 00:20:15.640 align:middle line:84%
So when I was giving
this talk to educators

00:20:15.640 --> 00:20:17.640 align:middle line:84%
seven or eight years ago
or something like that,

00:20:17.640 --> 00:20:19.940 align:middle line:84%
this would be the point
where I would say, imagine

00:20:19.940 --> 00:20:22.070 align:middle line:84%
you could go into
your school today,

00:20:22.070 --> 00:20:24.050 align:middle line:84%
imagine going into any
of the Cambridge Public

00:20:24.050 --> 00:20:26.175 align:middle line:84%
Schools, Somerville Public
Schools, whatever it is,

00:20:26.175 --> 00:20:27.830 align:middle line:84%
and grab every homework
assignment that

00:20:27.830 --> 00:20:29.330 align:middle line:90%
had been given out that day.

00:20:29.330 --> 00:20:32.470 align:middle line:84%
So go to all the classrooms,
collect every single assignment

00:20:32.470 --> 00:20:34.450 align:middle line:84%
that was given out, and
put them in a big pile

00:20:34.450 --> 00:20:36.250 align:middle line:90%
and start going through them.

00:20:36.250 --> 00:20:38.775 align:middle line:84%
Start classifying those
homework assignments,

00:20:38.775 --> 00:20:41.290 align:middle line:84%
like, how many of
them require some kind

00:20:41.290 --> 00:20:44.330 align:middle line:84%
of ill-structured problem
solving or expert thinking?

00:20:44.330 --> 00:20:47.160 align:middle line:84%
How many of them require some
kind of complex communication?

00:20:47.160 --> 00:20:48.910 align:middle line:84%
And how many of those
homework assignments

00:20:48.910 --> 00:20:51.640 align:middle line:84%
ask people to do the
kinds of routine work

00:20:51.640 --> 00:20:54.100 align:middle line:84%
that we no longer need
human beings to do?

00:20:54.100 --> 00:20:56.653 align:middle line:84%
And most educators
confronted with that question

00:20:56.653 --> 00:20:58.570 align:middle line:84%
would be like, I think
we're doing a whole lot

00:20:58.570 --> 00:21:00.910 align:middle line:90%
of the routine stuff.

00:21:00.910 --> 00:21:03.700 align:middle line:84%
And conceivably,
that's something

00:21:03.700 --> 00:21:08.200 align:middle line:84%
that we should be revisiting
as the world is changing.

00:21:08.200 --> 00:21:11.950 align:middle line:84%
How many of you encountered
the idea anywhere in your study

00:21:11.950 --> 00:21:14.770 align:middle line:84%
of education of
21st century skills?

00:21:14.770 --> 00:21:16.938 align:middle line:84%
Is that a thing that
people still discuss?

00:21:16.938 --> 00:21:18.980 align:middle line:84%
Courtney, when you were
ever working on products,

00:21:18.980 --> 00:21:22.420 align:middle line:84%
did people talk about 21st
century skills in your--

00:21:22.420 --> 00:21:26.208 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: No, I was mostly
in ELA and medical ed.

00:21:26.208 --> 00:21:27.250 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Yeah, yeah.

00:21:27.250 --> 00:21:28.190 align:middle line:84%
We might have had
some of this stuff.

00:21:28.190 --> 00:21:29.600 align:middle line:90%
Where did you encounter--

00:21:29.600 --> 00:21:33.950 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So in the nonprofit
space in India, for education,

00:21:33.950 --> 00:21:37.200 align:middle line:84%
there's a lot of nonprofits that
are trying to do 21st century

00:21:37.200 --> 00:21:37.700 align:middle line:90%
skills.

00:21:37.700 --> 00:21:40.498 align:middle line:84%
It was a buzzword, I
think, a while ago--

00:21:40.498 --> 00:21:41.290 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Yeah.

00:21:41.290 --> 00:21:43.430 align:middle line:84%
Once you're 25 years
into a century,

00:21:43.430 --> 00:21:46.100 align:middle line:84%
the idea of 21st
century skills becomes--

00:21:46.100 --> 00:21:49.183 align:middle line:84%
it felt more urgent
in 2004, 2005.

00:21:49.183 --> 00:21:50.600 align:middle line:84%
Where were you
going to say, Kirk?

00:21:50.600 --> 00:21:52.433 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I was just--
examining in general,

00:21:52.433 --> 00:21:53.738 align:middle line:90%
I think, came out of that.

00:21:53.738 --> 00:21:54.530 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Yeah.

00:21:54.530 --> 00:21:57.880 align:middle line:84%
There was this notion that,
OK, now that we have computers

00:21:57.880 --> 00:22:01.780 align:middle line:84%
saturating our workforce,
there's some new set of skills

00:22:01.780 --> 00:22:05.920 align:middle line:84%
that we're going to have to
teach young people how to do.

00:22:05.920 --> 00:22:10.548 align:middle line:84%
And it was kind of
interesting because if you--

00:22:10.548 --> 00:22:11.090 align:middle line:90%
I don't know.

00:22:11.090 --> 00:22:13.632 align:middle line:84%
There were probably like a dozen
different organizations that

00:22:13.632 --> 00:22:15.730 align:middle line:84%
came up with lists of
21st century skills.

00:22:15.730 --> 00:22:19.300 align:middle line:84%
For a while, there was this
thing called the Five Cs--

00:22:19.300 --> 00:22:23.900 align:middle line:84%
communication, collaboration,
creativity, critical thinking.

00:22:23.900 --> 00:22:24.830 align:middle line:90%
There's a fifth one.

00:22:24.830 --> 00:22:26.260 align:middle line:90%
Maybe it was the Four Cs.

00:22:26.260 --> 00:22:28.952 align:middle line:84%
I don't know, Four Cs,
Five Cs, something-- hmm?

00:22:28.952 --> 00:22:30.420 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Which one?

00:22:30.420 --> 00:22:32.560 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: The Four
Cs, something like that.

00:22:32.560 --> 00:22:34.740 align:middle line:84%
It was interesting because
actually, almost all

00:22:34.740 --> 00:22:37.830 align:middle line:84%
of those lists at some point
cite Murnane and Levy's work.

00:22:37.830 --> 00:22:41.040 align:middle line:84%
The only people that did
original, empirical analysis

00:22:41.040 --> 00:22:42.490 align:middle line:90%
were these two economists.

00:22:42.490 --> 00:22:45.690 align:middle line:84%
And then everyone else kind
of freestyled on top of that.

00:22:45.690 --> 00:22:50.880 align:middle line:84%
But I think there was something
significant there that you

00:22:50.880 --> 00:22:52.180 align:middle line:90%
could kind of tell by--

00:22:52.180 --> 00:22:54.720 align:middle line:84%
I mean, I think The
New Division of Labor

00:22:54.720 --> 00:22:56.533 align:middle line:90%
is 2005 or something like that.

00:22:56.533 --> 00:22:58.200 align:middle line:84%
But 20 years ago, you
could have a sense

00:22:58.200 --> 00:23:05.430 align:middle line:84%
that, man, we're going to need
many, many more people with far

00:23:05.430 --> 00:23:08.610 align:middle line:84%
more cognitive skills than
we've ever needed before

00:23:08.610 --> 00:23:10.450 align:middle line:90%
to fill our labor market.

00:23:10.450 --> 00:23:12.210 align:middle line:84%
We're just going to
need more people who

00:23:12.210 --> 00:23:14.710 align:middle line:84%
can do expert thinking,
ill-structured problem-solving,

00:23:14.710 --> 00:23:16.290 align:middle line:84%
complex communication
than we've ever

00:23:16.290 --> 00:23:19.470 align:middle line:84%
needed before in the history of
the American education system.

00:23:19.470 --> 00:23:21.940 align:middle line:84%
And that's a stressful
thing to contemplate.

00:23:21.940 --> 00:23:26.400 align:middle line:84%
So that's one set of fundamental
ideas, a way of thinking about,

00:23:26.400 --> 00:23:29.020 align:middle line:90%
what do we want people to know?

00:23:29.020 --> 00:23:32.590 align:middle line:84%
A kind of analysis
that people will

00:23:32.590 --> 00:23:33.920 align:middle line:90%
be doing in the labor market--

00:23:33.920 --> 00:23:36.370 align:middle line:84%
education economists,
labor market economists--

00:23:36.370 --> 00:23:38.630 align:middle line:84%
in the next five years is
something like, well, OK,

00:23:38.630 --> 00:23:41.170 align:middle line:84%
how do LLMs change
this equation?

00:23:41.170 --> 00:23:43.520 align:middle line:84%
How does generative AI
change this equation?

00:23:43.520 --> 00:23:46.880 align:middle line:84%
Does it simply accelerate trends
that have already been existing?

00:23:46.880 --> 00:23:52.240 align:middle line:84%
Is there some new set of
answers to these questions?

00:23:52.240 --> 00:23:56.112 align:middle line:84%
Are there different
classes of skills?

00:23:56.112 --> 00:23:58.570 align:middle line:84%
David Autor, who's one of the
people I just mentioned, just

00:23:58.570 --> 00:23:59.560 align:middle line:90%
wrote a--

00:23:59.560 --> 00:24:01.240 align:middle line:90%
I don't know what he wrote.

00:24:01.240 --> 00:24:02.980 align:middle line:84%
I think he wrote a
working paper that

00:24:02.980 --> 00:24:06.430 align:middle line:84%
was then quoted in The New York
Times or something like that.

00:24:06.430 --> 00:24:09.100 align:middle line:84%
He's been a person who, for
decades, has been saying,

00:24:09.100 --> 00:24:11.740 align:middle line:84%
oh, computers are pretty
bad for the middle class.

00:24:11.740 --> 00:24:14.620 align:middle line:90%
A figure like this--

00:24:14.620 --> 00:24:16.630 align:middle line:84%
this is a bad figure
for the middle class.

00:24:16.630 --> 00:24:18.480 align:middle line:84%
This is where the
middle class existed.

00:24:18.480 --> 00:24:19.730 align:middle line:90%
These people are disappearing.

00:24:19.730 --> 00:24:22.220 align:middle line:84%
These are either rich folks
getting richer or poor folks--

00:24:22.220 --> 00:24:24.400 align:middle line:84%
the service line is poor
folks getting poorer.

00:24:24.400 --> 00:24:26.510 align:middle line:84%
But he just recently
came out with something

00:24:26.510 --> 00:24:30.027 align:middle line:84%
which is like, actually,
LLMs are going to be

00:24:30.027 --> 00:24:31.110 align:middle line:90%
good for the middle class.

00:24:31.110 --> 00:24:33.200 align:middle line:84%
Generative AI is going to
be good for the middle class

00:24:33.200 --> 00:24:35.270 align:middle line:84%
because there's going to
be a whole class of jobs

00:24:35.270 --> 00:24:37.905 align:middle line:84%
that people can
do with AI support

00:24:37.905 --> 00:24:39.530 align:middle line:84%
that they wouldn't
have been able to do

00:24:39.530 --> 00:24:42.300 align:middle line:84%
without graduate training
or something like that.

00:24:42.300 --> 00:24:45.080 align:middle line:84%
And now they're going to be able
to do it with these systems that

00:24:45.080 --> 00:24:46.370 align:middle line:90%
are helping them.

00:24:46.370 --> 00:24:50.330 align:middle line:84%
And then there's another
economist here, Daron Acemoglu,

00:24:50.330 --> 00:24:51.920 align:middle line:84%
who was like, I
don't know, David.

00:24:51.920 --> 00:24:53.420 align:middle line:84%
In The New York
Times' article, he's

00:24:53.420 --> 00:24:54.780 align:middle line:84%
like, oh, that's
pretty optimistic.

00:24:54.780 --> 00:24:56.270 align:middle line:84%
I'm not sure that's
going to work out.

00:24:56.270 --> 00:24:57.560 align:middle line:84%
But these are the kinds
of things that people

00:24:57.560 --> 00:24:58.518 align:middle line:90%
are wrestling with now.

00:24:58.518 --> 00:25:01.400 align:middle line:90%


00:25:01.400 --> 00:25:05.630 align:middle line:84%
The heart of the trap
of routine assessment

00:25:05.630 --> 00:25:09.740 align:middle line:84%
is that the kinds of
things that computers

00:25:09.740 --> 00:25:12.560 align:middle line:84%
are good at assessing
almost exactly

00:25:12.560 --> 00:25:15.080 align:middle line:84%
overlap with the kinds
of things that computers

00:25:15.080 --> 00:25:16.482 align:middle line:90%
are good at doing.

00:25:16.482 --> 00:25:18.440 align:middle line:84%
And those are the kinds
of things that we don't

00:25:18.440 --> 00:25:20.330 align:middle line:90%
need people to do anymore.

00:25:20.330 --> 00:25:22.530 align:middle line:84%
The kinds of things
that we can build,

00:25:22.530 --> 00:25:25.630 align:middle line:84%
tools that are good at
assessing human performance

00:25:25.630 --> 00:25:29.670 align:middle line:84%
are almost always very closely
overlapping with the kinds

00:25:29.670 --> 00:25:32.170 align:middle line:84%
of things that computers are
good at and the kinds of things

00:25:32.170 --> 00:25:33.587 align:middle line:84%
you don't need
them to do anymore.

00:25:33.587 --> 00:25:35.590 align:middle line:84%
So here are a
couple of examples.

00:25:35.590 --> 00:25:39.320 align:middle line:84%
Here's a framework for
mathematical modeling.

00:25:39.320 --> 00:25:42.580 align:middle line:84%
So one of the big changes in
the Common Core State Standards

00:25:42.580 --> 00:25:45.340 align:middle line:84%
that came about 15 years
ago or something like that

00:25:45.340 --> 00:25:48.870 align:middle line:84%
was that we wanted to
teach students not just

00:25:48.870 --> 00:25:50.870 align:middle line:84%
to do procedural kinds
of things in mathematics,

00:25:50.870 --> 00:25:52.420 align:middle line:84%
but to be able to
conduct exercises

00:25:52.420 --> 00:25:53.660 align:middle line:90%
in mathematical modeling.

00:25:53.660 --> 00:25:56.320 align:middle line:84%
And those exercises of
mathematical modeling,

00:25:56.320 --> 00:25:58.250 align:middle line:84%
you could think of
them having five steps.

00:25:58.250 --> 00:26:00.790 align:middle line:84%
First, you have to find
some problem in the world.

00:26:00.790 --> 00:26:02.420 align:middle line:84%
What is an interesting
thing to view?

00:26:02.420 --> 00:26:04.750 align:middle line:84%
Then you have to set
that up as a model,

00:26:04.750 --> 00:26:07.780 align:middle line:84%
whether that's an equation,
or a schematic, or a, table

00:26:07.780 --> 00:26:08.570 align:middle line:90%
or whatever it is.

00:26:08.570 --> 00:26:09.580 align:middle line:84%
You have to take
a problem and then

00:26:09.580 --> 00:26:11.090 align:middle line:84%
you have to frame it
in some kind of way.

00:26:11.090 --> 00:26:13.280 align:middle line:84%
Then, probably once you've
developed that model,

00:26:13.280 --> 00:26:16.880 align:middle line:84%
you have to compute some kind
of solution inside that model.

00:26:16.880 --> 00:26:20.500 align:middle line:84%
Then you have to
explain your solution

00:26:20.500 --> 00:26:22.160 align:middle line:90%
in terms of the real world.

00:26:22.160 --> 00:26:24.910 align:middle line:84%
So was it 27 board feet,
or was it 27 kittens,

00:26:24.910 --> 00:26:27.160 align:middle line:90%
or was it 27 acres?

00:26:27.160 --> 00:26:29.590 align:middle line:84%
And then you have to
be able to explain

00:26:29.590 --> 00:26:31.410 align:middle line:90%
why your solution makes sense.

00:26:31.410 --> 00:26:33.910 align:middle line:84%
Why did you follow a series of
reasonable mathematical steps

00:26:33.910 --> 00:26:35.470 align:middle line:90%
to come up with that solution?

00:26:35.470 --> 00:26:39.610 align:middle line:84%
Of those roughly five
steps, what do we

00:26:39.610 --> 00:26:41.416 align:middle line:90%
have technologies to evaluate?

00:26:41.416 --> 00:26:47.500 align:middle line:90%


00:26:47.500 --> 00:26:50.890 align:middle line:84%
Of those five things, what
have you ever been tested on?

00:26:50.890 --> 00:26:52.130 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: I want to say 3.

00:26:52.130 --> 00:26:54.920 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: Yeah,
computing the solution.

00:26:54.920 --> 00:26:57.790 align:middle line:84%
The only thing of those five
steps that we can reliably

00:26:57.790 --> 00:27:00.640 align:middle line:84%
get computers to do is
evaluate whether or not

00:27:00.640 --> 00:27:02.060 align:middle line:90%
you can compute a solution.

00:27:02.060 --> 00:27:05.030 align:middle line:84%
If you use mathematics
in your profession,

00:27:05.030 --> 00:27:09.280 align:middle line:84%
which of those five steps will
you almost never actually do?

00:27:09.280 --> 00:27:11.980 align:middle line:90%
Computing the solution.

00:27:11.980 --> 00:27:14.390 align:middle line:84%
A computer can't tell you
which problems are interesting.

00:27:14.390 --> 00:27:17.080 align:middle line:84%
A computer can't tell you like,
oh, this would be a good problem

00:27:17.080 --> 00:27:18.955 align:middle line:84%
to think about through
this kind of equation,

00:27:18.955 --> 00:27:22.440 align:middle line:84%
or through this kind of formula,
or through this kind of framing.

00:27:22.440 --> 00:27:24.588 align:middle line:84%
A computer is not that
good at taking the outputs

00:27:24.588 --> 00:27:27.130 align:middle line:84%
and being like, OK, this is what
it means for the real world.

00:27:27.130 --> 00:27:28.838 align:middle line:84%
And computers are
really not particularly

00:27:28.838 --> 00:27:31.320 align:middle line:84%
good at explaining to you
why what just happened

00:27:31.320 --> 00:27:32.470 align:middle line:90%
mathematically makes sense.

00:27:32.470 --> 00:27:34.650 align:middle line:84%
But the one thing that
you will assuredly

00:27:34.650 --> 00:27:36.810 align:middle line:84%
do if you're a
professional mathematician

00:27:36.810 --> 00:27:38.760 align:middle line:84%
is have the computer
do the math.

00:27:38.760 --> 00:27:41.550 align:middle line:84%
The computers are
significantly more reliable

00:27:41.550 --> 00:27:44.580 align:middle line:84%
than you are at
those kinds of steps.

00:27:44.580 --> 00:27:49.650 align:middle line:84%
So if you go and sit down for
any mathematics exam that's

00:27:49.650 --> 00:27:52.770 align:middle line:84%
given in a standardized
way at any level,

00:27:52.770 --> 00:27:56.670 align:middle line:84%
you will spend the vast
majority of your time computing.

00:27:56.670 --> 00:27:59.228 align:middle line:84%
If you're a mathematician,
you'll spend almost-- not really

00:27:59.228 --> 00:28:01.770 align:middle line:84%
a mathematician, but if you are
a person who uses mathematics

00:28:01.770 --> 00:28:03.660 align:middle line:84%
in their work, you will spend
almost none of your time

00:28:03.660 --> 00:28:04.197 align:middle line:90%
computing.

00:28:04.197 --> 00:28:06.030 align:middle line:84%
You will spend almost
all of your time doing

00:28:06.030 --> 00:28:07.560 align:middle line:90%
those other kinds of things.

00:28:07.560 --> 00:28:09.160 align:middle line:90%
That's a real problem.

00:28:09.160 --> 00:28:11.070 align:middle line:90%
That's a real problem.

00:28:11.070 --> 00:28:17.220 align:middle line:84%
A useful concept that
people who study--

00:28:17.220 --> 00:28:19.650 align:middle line:84%
psychometricians, people
who study testing,

00:28:19.650 --> 00:28:23.400 align:middle line:84%
they have a term for how we
misunderstand math tests,

00:28:23.400 --> 00:28:26.490 align:middle line:84%
or tests in general, and it's
called the reification fallacy.

00:28:26.490 --> 00:28:29.670 align:middle line:84%
Reification means the
naming of something.

00:28:29.670 --> 00:28:32.170 align:middle line:84%
If you call something
a math test,

00:28:32.170 --> 00:28:34.150 align:middle line:84%
people will think
it's a math test.

00:28:34.150 --> 00:28:35.730 align:middle line:84%
But calling
something a math test

00:28:35.730 --> 00:28:37.440 align:middle line:90%
doesn't make it a math test.

00:28:37.440 --> 00:28:39.580 align:middle line:84%
So for instance, every
time you take a math test,

00:28:39.580 --> 00:28:41.200 align:middle line:84%
you need to know
something about language.

00:28:41.200 --> 00:28:42.658 align:middle line:84%
You probably need
to know something

00:28:42.658 --> 00:28:44.070 align:middle line:90%
about test-taking strategies.

00:28:44.070 --> 00:28:46.140 align:middle line:84%
There's probably a whole
bunch of other cultural things

00:28:46.140 --> 00:28:47.530 align:middle line:84%
that you need to know
to function in a test.

00:28:47.530 --> 00:28:50.250 align:middle line:84%
And the test is effectively
evaluating all those things.

00:28:50.250 --> 00:28:51.990 align:middle line:90%
The other thing is a test can--

00:28:51.990 --> 00:28:55.290 align:middle line:84%
there's no test that can
say that all of eighth grade

00:28:55.290 --> 00:28:56.640 align:middle line:90%
algebra.

00:28:56.640 --> 00:28:58.920 align:middle line:90%
That would take you forever.

00:28:58.920 --> 00:29:01.315 align:middle line:84%
Instead, what we do is we
sample out of a domain.

00:29:01.315 --> 00:29:02.940 align:middle line:84%
We say, here's all
the stuff that might

00:29:02.940 --> 00:29:04.360 align:middle line:90%
fit in eighth grade algebra.

00:29:04.360 --> 00:29:06.720 align:middle line:84%
And we're going to pick
some of this stuff,

00:29:06.720 --> 00:29:08.713 align:middle line:84%
and some of this stuff,
and some of this stuff.

00:29:08.713 --> 00:29:10.380 align:middle line:84%
A way you could think
about this problem

00:29:10.380 --> 00:29:14.133 align:middle line:84%
is that we oversample computing,
and we undersample all

00:29:14.133 --> 00:29:15.425 align:middle line:90%
of these other kinds of things.

00:29:15.425 --> 00:29:17.970 align:middle line:90%


00:29:17.970 --> 00:29:21.470 align:middle line:84%
Well, maybe let's do one
more that's like this.

00:29:21.470 --> 00:29:23.870 align:middle line:84%
Here's one of my very
favorite paragraphs

00:29:23.870 --> 00:29:26.270 align:middle line:84%
that's ever been written
about computer programming

00:29:26.270 --> 00:29:29.600 align:middle line:84%
by Hal Abelson, who's here,
and some of his colleagues.

00:29:29.600 --> 00:29:33.620 align:middle line:84%
This is from a computer
science textbook.

00:29:33.620 --> 00:29:36.590 align:middle line:84%
"First we want to establish the
idea that computer language is

00:29:36.590 --> 00:29:39.960 align:middle line:84%
not just a way of getting a
computer to perform operations,

00:29:39.960 --> 00:29:42.860 align:middle line:84%
but rather that it is a novel,
formal medium for expressing

00:29:42.860 --> 00:29:44.603 align:middle line:90%
ideas about methodology."

00:29:44.603 --> 00:29:46.020 align:middle line:84%
This is probably
the key sentence.

00:29:46.020 --> 00:29:49.140 align:middle line:84%
"Thus, programs must be
written for people to read,

00:29:49.140 --> 00:29:52.123 align:middle line:84%
and only incidentally
for machines to execute.

00:29:52.123 --> 00:29:54.290 align:middle line:84%
Second, we believe that the
essential material to be

00:29:54.290 --> 00:29:56.360 align:middle line:84%
addressed by a subject of
this level is not the syntax

00:29:56.360 --> 00:29:58.360 align:middle line:84%
of a particular programming
language constructs,

00:29:58.360 --> 00:30:00.960 align:middle line:84%
nor clever algorithms for
computing particular functions,

00:30:00.960 --> 00:30:02.960 align:middle line:84%
nor even the mathematical
analysis of algorithms

00:30:02.960 --> 00:30:05.570 align:middle line:84%
and foundations of computing,
but rather the techniques used

00:30:05.570 --> 00:30:09.920 align:middle line:84%
to control the intellectual
complexity of large software

00:30:09.920 --> 00:30:11.330 align:middle line:90%
systems."

00:30:11.330 --> 00:30:13.610 align:middle line:84%
Why would it be
important for programs

00:30:13.610 --> 00:30:16.110 align:middle line:84%
to be written for people to
read and only incidentally

00:30:16.110 --> 00:30:18.982 align:middle line:90%
for machines to run?

00:30:18.982 --> 00:30:21.440 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: People are the ones
who are maintaining the code.

00:30:21.440 --> 00:30:22.820 align:middle line:84%
If they can't
understand it, then how

00:30:22.820 --> 00:30:23.790 align:middle line:90%
are they going to maintain it?

00:30:23.790 --> 00:30:25.050 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: People have
to be able to maintain it.

00:30:25.050 --> 00:30:26.440 align:middle line:90%
What else might folks say?

00:30:26.440 --> 00:30:31.100 align:middle line:90%


00:30:31.100 --> 00:30:32.832 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, the way you
add new features,

00:30:32.832 --> 00:30:35.540 align:middle line:84%
the way you collaborate, the way
you build large, complex systems

00:30:35.540 --> 00:30:38.123 align:middle line:84%
is that you build a system that
people can read and understand

00:30:38.123 --> 00:30:39.205 align:middle line:90%
what it's doing.

00:30:39.205 --> 00:30:41.780 align:middle line:84%
What you can't read and can't
understand what it's doing.

00:30:41.780 --> 00:30:47.120 align:middle line:84%
Now, when you all take
introductory computing classes,

00:30:47.120 --> 00:30:49.490 align:middle line:84%
and you submit your
homework assignments,

00:30:49.490 --> 00:30:51.090 align:middle line:84%
and they're
automatically graded,

00:30:51.090 --> 00:30:53.120 align:middle line:84%
what are the characteristics
of your programming

00:30:53.120 --> 00:30:56.492 align:middle line:84%
that those systems
are good at grading?

00:30:56.492 --> 00:30:59.330 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: The output of
whatever the code is.

00:30:59.330 --> 00:31:01.280 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: OK,
so the assignment

00:31:01.280 --> 00:31:03.350 align:middle line:84%
presupposes some kind
of engineering test

00:31:03.350 --> 00:31:05.650 align:middle line:84%
and figures out whether
or not you pass that test.

00:31:05.650 --> 00:31:08.810 align:middle line:84%
Did your spell check dictionary
detect the misspelled words

00:31:08.810 --> 00:31:11.540 align:middle line:90%
or not?

00:31:11.540 --> 00:31:14.570 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: If it passed a
series of randomized test cases

00:31:14.570 --> 00:31:15.445 align:middle line:90%
from input to output.

00:31:15.445 --> 00:31:16.403 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: OK, good.

00:31:16.403 --> 00:31:17.740 align:middle line:90%
So similar kind of thing.

00:31:17.740 --> 00:31:20.540 align:middle line:84%
We throw a we throw a bunch of
possible inputs in this system.

00:31:20.540 --> 00:31:21.670 align:middle line:84%
Does it all come
up with the outputs

00:31:21.670 --> 00:31:22.910 align:middle line:84%
that it's supposed
to come up with?

00:31:22.910 --> 00:31:24.285 align:middle line:84%
Other kinds of
engineering tests.

00:31:24.285 --> 00:31:25.113 align:middle line:90%
Dana?

00:31:25.113 --> 00:31:26.530 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I was
just going to say,

00:31:26.530 --> 00:31:30.190 align:middle line:90%
sometimes isn't that a time?

00:31:30.190 --> 00:31:31.482 align:middle line:90%
Does your code run fast enough?

00:31:31.482 --> 00:31:32.273 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Good.

00:31:32.273 --> 00:31:33.220 align:middle line:90%
So there could be a--

00:31:33.220 --> 00:31:34.885 align:middle line:84%
that's one kind of
efficiency metric.

00:31:34.885 --> 00:31:37.510 align:middle line:84%
I think there are other kind of
efficiency metrics that maybe--

00:31:37.510 --> 00:31:39.100 align:middle line:84%
have you ever done
things where you only

00:31:39.100 --> 00:31:41.183 align:middle line:84%
have a certain number of
lines or a certain number

00:31:41.183 --> 00:31:43.190 align:middle line:84%
of characters or something
like that to try to--

00:31:43.190 --> 00:31:43.690 align:middle line:90%
hmm?

00:31:43.690 --> 00:31:44.470 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: That's some--

00:31:44.470 --> 00:31:45.970 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: Generate
some parsimony or something

00:31:45.970 --> 00:31:46.520 align:middle line:90%
like that.

00:31:46.520 --> 00:31:50.750 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Some classes here
do have these style checkers.

00:31:50.750 --> 00:31:53.510 align:middle line:84%
I'm not too sure what
they check, exactly.

00:31:53.510 --> 00:31:57.220 align:middle line:84%
But they check for certain
like stylistic choices

00:31:57.220 --> 00:31:58.520 align:middle line:90%
you made in your coding.

00:31:58.520 --> 00:32:00.880 align:middle line:84%
But a lot of them
also depend on you

00:32:00.880 --> 00:32:05.680 align:middle line:84%
to have correct computational
code to then give you

00:32:05.680 --> 00:32:06.508 align:middle line:90%
the style point.

00:32:06.508 --> 00:32:07.550 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Yeah, good.

00:32:07.550 --> 00:32:09.820 align:middle line:84%
And I think the style
starts getting at,

00:32:09.820 --> 00:32:12.168 align:middle line:84%
the programs must be
written for people to read.

00:32:12.168 --> 00:32:13.710 align:middle line:84%
Now, they probably
don't check things

00:32:13.710 --> 00:32:17.018 align:middle line:84%
like, Did you name your
variables and your functions

00:32:17.018 --> 00:32:18.810 align:middle line:84%
something sensible that
somebody else would

00:32:18.810 --> 00:32:21.630 align:middle line:84%
be able to understand later
on? or other kinds of things

00:32:21.630 --> 00:32:22.530 align:middle line:90%
like that.

00:32:22.530 --> 00:32:28.050 align:middle line:84%
But you could think of those
style guides as initial--

00:32:28.050 --> 00:32:31.780 align:middle line:84%
we have pretty good tests
for the "incidentally,

00:32:31.780 --> 00:32:33.160 align:middle line:90%
for machines to execute."

00:32:33.160 --> 00:32:38.394 align:middle line:84%
That's kind of our efficacy,
our efficiency evaluations.

00:32:38.394 --> 00:32:41.983 align:middle line:84%
In some ways, the style checkers
you're describing, also,

00:32:41.983 --> 00:32:43.150 align:middle line:90%
they might be kind of silly.

00:32:43.150 --> 00:32:47.070 align:middle line:84%
But another way to think of them
is as a reasonable first step

00:32:47.070 --> 00:32:48.600 align:middle line:90%
towards these kinds of things.

00:32:48.600 --> 00:32:50.580 align:middle line:84%
What are all the
machine-readable parts

00:32:50.580 --> 00:32:54.040 align:middle line:84%
of style that we can get
a computer to evaluate?

00:32:54.040 --> 00:32:58.950 align:middle line:84%
And how, over time, would we
build increasingly sophisticated

00:32:58.950 --> 00:33:01.620 align:middle line:84%
style checkers, computer
assessors that would just

00:33:01.620 --> 00:33:05.730 align:middle line:84%
do a better and better job
year after year of evaluating

00:33:05.730 --> 00:33:08.452 align:middle line:84%
more and more features of code
that have to do with humans

00:33:08.452 --> 00:33:10.410 align:middle line:84%
understanding and
communicating with each other

00:33:10.410 --> 00:33:11.702 align:middle line:90%
rather than with just machines?

00:33:11.702 --> 00:33:13.200 align:middle line:90%
What were you going to say?

00:33:13.200 --> 00:33:16.525 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I was just going
to add that basically, I've

00:33:16.525 --> 00:33:18.150 align:middle line:84%
been a part of a
course where they also

00:33:18.150 --> 00:33:21.840 align:middle line:84%
do design checks for the
code on top of style metrics.

00:33:21.840 --> 00:33:25.240 align:middle line:84%
So they're checking, are you
writing good, efficient loops?

00:33:25.240 --> 00:33:26.830 align:middle line:90%
Are you writing redundant code?

00:33:26.830 --> 00:33:31.050 align:middle line:84%
But even so, I really feel
that, within this introductory

00:33:31.050 --> 00:33:33.240 align:middle line:84%
programming space,
they're mostly

00:33:33.240 --> 00:33:36.000 align:middle line:84%
checking for how well the
students are overfitting

00:33:36.000 --> 00:33:38.740 align:middle line:84%
to the given problem
space already.

00:33:38.740 --> 00:33:41.710 align:middle line:84%
What they want to test
for is problem-solving,

00:33:41.710 --> 00:33:43.548 align:middle line:84%
but that's just really
hard to do, I think.

00:33:43.548 --> 00:33:44.340 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Good.

00:33:44.340 --> 00:33:47.440 align:middle line:90%
So that it's very difficult--

00:33:47.440 --> 00:33:50.560 align:middle line:84%
so A, in the solution
of any given problem,

00:33:50.560 --> 00:33:52.710 align:middle line:84%
it's very difficult to
detect for something

00:33:52.710 --> 00:33:55.750 align:middle line:84%
that would be generalizable
problem-solving skills.

00:33:55.750 --> 00:33:58.650 align:middle line:84%
And even worse, it's
essentially-- so far,

00:33:58.650 --> 00:34:01.920 align:middle line:84%
it's proven more or less
impossible to design assessment

00:34:01.920 --> 00:34:04.260 align:middle line:84%
machines that can
detect something

00:34:04.260 --> 00:34:06.450 align:middle line:84%
like generalized
problem solving skills.

00:34:06.450 --> 00:34:09.870 align:middle line:84%
To some extent, anytime we
build these things, our most

00:34:09.870 --> 00:34:14.489 align:middle line:84%
sophisticated assessment tools,
work best on particular problems

00:34:14.489 --> 00:34:17.540 align:middle line:84%
that you've fit them to work
on, and other kinds of things

00:34:17.540 --> 00:34:18.040 align:middle line:90%
like that.

00:34:18.040 --> 00:34:22.830 align:middle line:84%
And they work less well on more
generalizable kinds of things.

00:34:22.830 --> 00:34:27.045 align:middle line:84%
What are some other domains that
we could think of that are--

00:34:27.045 --> 00:34:30.120 align:middle line:84%
maybe I'll have you chat
with each other for a minute.

00:34:30.120 --> 00:34:32.460 align:middle line:90%
Talk about two things.

00:34:32.460 --> 00:34:35.310 align:middle line:84%
What are just other
domains that you

00:34:35.310 --> 00:34:39.179 align:middle line:84%
can think of that are like
this, where computers can test

00:34:39.179 --> 00:34:42.360 align:middle line:84%
something in the domain, but
they may not be able to capture

00:34:42.360 --> 00:34:45.960 align:middle line:84%
the most important or big,
important classes of things

00:34:45.960 --> 00:34:47.362 align:middle line:90%
in that domain?

00:34:47.362 --> 00:34:48.820 align:middle line:84%
Think about that
for a few minutes.

00:34:48.820 --> 00:34:51.120 align:middle line:84%
That might be putting
a pessimist's hat

00:34:51.120 --> 00:34:52.290 align:middle line:90%
on to think about that.

00:34:52.290 --> 00:34:56.200 align:middle line:84%
And then put on your optimist
hat for a minute or two and say,

00:34:56.200 --> 00:34:58.830 align:middle line:84%
in the next five
years, where do you

00:34:58.830 --> 00:35:04.140 align:middle line:84%
think we're most likely to see
the most progress in thwarting

00:35:04.140 --> 00:35:05.880 align:middle line:90%
the trap of routine assessment?

00:35:05.880 --> 00:35:10.540 align:middle line:84%
Where are we most likely to
develop assessment tools that

00:35:10.540 --> 00:35:13.810 align:middle line:84%
actually evaluate people on
things that we really care about

00:35:13.810 --> 00:35:15.693 align:middle line:84%
and want human beings
to be able to do?

00:35:15.693 --> 00:35:17.860 align:middle line:84%
Turn to the person next to
you or a couple of people

00:35:17.860 --> 00:35:20.090 align:middle line:84%
and talk about those two things
for three or four minutes.

00:35:20.090 --> 00:35:20.380 align:middle line:90%
Ready?

00:35:20.380 --> 00:35:20.880 align:middle line:90%
Go.

00:35:20.880 --> 00:35:23.560 align:middle line:90%


00:35:23.560 --> 00:35:25.510 align:middle line:90%
Let's do the first one first.

00:35:25.510 --> 00:35:27.640 align:middle line:84%
What are some domains
that you could

00:35:27.640 --> 00:35:30.298 align:middle line:84%
think of where it
seemed like, ah,

00:35:30.298 --> 00:35:32.590 align:middle line:84%
we really are having a hard
time using computers to get

00:35:32.590 --> 00:35:36.250 align:middle line:90%
at the important stuff here?

00:35:36.250 --> 00:35:38.410 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Essay writing
or short stories

00:35:38.410 --> 00:35:40.940 align:middle line:84%
or anything to do with
writing and literature.

00:35:40.940 --> 00:35:41.800 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Great.

00:35:41.800 --> 00:35:47.030 align:middle line:84%
And that's probably one
of the most studied,

00:35:47.030 --> 00:35:49.460 align:middle line:90%
most examined domains.

00:35:49.460 --> 00:35:52.150 align:middle line:84%
And again, it's a
place where we have--

00:35:52.150 --> 00:35:57.310 align:middle line:84%
at least the baseline in that
field is pretty well understood.

00:35:57.310 --> 00:36:00.492 align:middle line:84%
So some of the very
worst assessment--

00:36:00.492 --> 00:36:04.280 align:middle line:84%
just the most disappointing
kind of assessments we do

00:36:04.280 --> 00:36:08.620 align:middle line:84%
is people's long-form essay
writing on standardized tests.

00:36:08.620 --> 00:36:11.140 align:middle line:84%
There was a guy here
named Les Perelman

00:36:11.140 --> 00:36:16.420 align:middle line:84%
who used to joke about the
quality of standardized writing

00:36:16.420 --> 00:36:20.920 align:middle line:84%
assessment by noting that he
could pretty reliably score

00:36:20.920 --> 00:36:23.440 align:middle line:84%
essays from across the room
because you just hold them

00:36:23.440 --> 00:36:25.000 align:middle line:90%
up and see how long they are.

00:36:25.000 --> 00:36:26.770 align:middle line:84%
And just by glancing
them, he could

00:36:26.770 --> 00:36:29.030 align:middle line:84%
be like, oh, that's 2,
that's a 3, that's a 5.

00:36:29.030 --> 00:36:32.073 align:middle line:84%
He also wrote pretty early on
a pretty fun piece of software.

00:36:32.073 --> 00:36:33.490 align:middle line:84%
There are all these
companies that

00:36:33.490 --> 00:36:35.090 align:middle line:84%
were creating automated
essay graders,

00:36:35.090 --> 00:36:36.730 align:middle line:84%
and so he got a
group of MIT students

00:36:36.730 --> 00:36:41.620 align:middle line:84%
to make software that generated
nonsense text that did really

00:36:41.620 --> 00:36:45.827 align:middle line:84%
well on these automated essay
scores, which was just-- it was

00:36:45.827 --> 00:36:47.410 align:middle line:84%
pretty fun to read
these things, which

00:36:47.410 --> 00:36:50.210 align:middle line:84%
just were like complete
hallucinations of madness.

00:36:50.210 --> 00:36:53.140 align:middle line:84%
And then some system
would be like, 5.

00:36:53.140 --> 00:36:59.470 align:middle line:84%
But we actually have
developed software tools, so

00:36:59.470 --> 00:37:02.540 align:middle line:84%
when GRE essays are scored
or things like that.

00:37:02.540 --> 00:37:04.010 align:middle line:90%
So humans go and score them.

00:37:04.010 --> 00:37:06.610 align:middle line:84%
It's incredibly disappointing
how they do that.

00:37:06.610 --> 00:37:08.350 align:middle line:90%
They read them all very quickly.

00:37:08.350 --> 00:37:09.640 align:middle line:90%
They score them holistically.

00:37:09.640 --> 00:37:12.880 align:middle line:84%
The people who do the scoring
are not particularly well-paid,

00:37:12.880 --> 00:37:15.120 align:middle line:84%
not particularly well-trained,
as you might imagine.

00:37:15.120 --> 00:37:17.910 align:middle line:84%
It's enormously boring to
read essays over and over

00:37:17.910 --> 00:37:20.910 align:middle line:84%
again about, Who is
the person in your life

00:37:20.910 --> 00:37:22.540 align:middle line:90%
that inspired you the most?

00:37:22.540 --> 00:37:27.360 align:middle line:84%
or whatever other ridiculous
questions they ask.

00:37:27.360 --> 00:37:29.160 align:middle line:84%
But anyway, you can
train those people

00:37:29.160 --> 00:37:34.620 align:middle line:84%
to be reasonably similar in
their scoring to one another.

00:37:34.620 --> 00:37:38.430 align:middle line:84%
And not only that, but for
a given question-- like,

00:37:38.430 --> 00:37:40.300 align:middle line:84%
if we ask people
over and over again,

00:37:40.300 --> 00:37:42.605 align:middle line:84%
Who is the person that
inspired you the most?

00:37:42.605 --> 00:37:44.230 align:middle line:84%
you have a bunch of
humans evaluate it,

00:37:44.230 --> 00:37:47.580 align:middle line:84%
you feed all of those essays
into some kind of machine

00:37:47.580 --> 00:37:52.620 align:middle line:84%
learning algorithm, we can build
machines that are roughly as

00:37:52.620 --> 00:37:55.110 align:middle line:90%
reliable as those humans.

00:37:55.110 --> 00:37:59.820 align:middle line:84%
So we can create automated
essay scoring tools

00:37:59.820 --> 00:38:01.990 align:middle line:90%
that do a reasonably good job--

00:38:01.990 --> 00:38:04.620 align:middle line:84%
that predict what a human
would score an essay

00:38:04.620 --> 00:38:08.085 align:middle line:84%
about as reliably as two
humans would predict what

00:38:08.085 --> 00:38:09.710 align:middle line:84%
the other person
would score the essay,

00:38:09.710 --> 00:38:12.690 align:middle line:84%
about as reliable
as two people are.

00:38:12.690 --> 00:38:16.130 align:middle line:84%
Those systems don't work
for shorter-answer things.

00:38:16.130 --> 00:38:19.430 align:middle line:84%
So you have to be in the
realm of a few hundred words

00:38:19.430 --> 00:38:21.180 align:middle line:84%
before these systems
become reliable.

00:38:21.180 --> 00:38:23.760 align:middle line:84%
They're also not very
useful for novel questions.

00:38:23.760 --> 00:38:25.550 align:middle line:84%
If you invent a
new essay question

00:38:25.550 --> 00:38:27.280 align:middle line:84%
you want to give
someone, you can't

00:38:27.280 --> 00:38:29.030 align:middle line:84%
take one of these
algorithms off the shelf

00:38:29.030 --> 00:38:33.440 align:middle line:84%
and run it against the system
and have it work, which is why--

00:38:33.440 --> 00:38:34.120 align:middle line:90%
I don't know.

00:38:34.120 --> 00:38:36.120 align:middle line:84%
I don't know how they do
it exactly with essays,

00:38:36.120 --> 00:38:39.650 align:middle line:84%
but these standardized test
companies, some fraction

00:38:39.650 --> 00:38:42.847 align:middle line:84%
of the tests that you take every
time you take it is not scored,

00:38:42.847 --> 00:38:44.930 align:middle line:84%
and it's just used for
development for these kinds

00:38:44.930 --> 00:38:45.980 align:middle line:90%
of things like that.

00:38:45.980 --> 00:38:48.200 align:middle line:84%
So you're somehow--
we've convinced

00:38:48.200 --> 00:38:50.630 align:middle line:84%
you to pay $75 to
do voluntary work

00:38:50.630 --> 00:38:54.650 align:middle line:84%
for giant test-making
companies and things like that.

00:38:54.650 --> 00:38:59.990 align:middle line:84%
But yeah, that is
a domain in which--

00:38:59.990 --> 00:39:01.142 align:middle line:90%
people will also disagree.

00:39:01.142 --> 00:39:02.600 align:middle line:84%
Some people will
be like, man, it's

00:39:02.600 --> 00:39:05.330 align:middle line:84%
great that we have these systems
because if you have tools that

00:39:05.330 --> 00:39:07.490 align:middle line:84%
can automatically
score essays, then

00:39:07.490 --> 00:39:09.720 align:middle line:84%
you can encourage people
to write more essays.

00:39:09.720 --> 00:39:13.555 align:middle line:84%
If we didn't have these machines
that could score essays,

00:39:13.555 --> 00:39:15.180 align:middle line:84%
they wouldn't be on
standardized tests.

00:39:15.180 --> 00:39:16.650 align:middle line:84%
And if wouldn't be on
standardized tests,

00:39:16.650 --> 00:39:17.730 align:middle line:84%
then teachers
wouldn't assign them.

00:39:17.730 --> 00:39:18.930 align:middle line:84%
And then we would
teach less writing.

00:39:18.930 --> 00:39:21.740 align:middle line:84%
And so as crappy as they-- maybe
there are some critiques about

00:39:21.740 --> 00:39:22.380 align:middle line:90%
their concern--

00:39:22.380 --> 00:39:24.463 align:middle line:84%
maybe there are some
concerns about their quality,

00:39:24.463 --> 00:39:26.840 align:middle line:84%
but they're still
good for the system.

00:39:26.840 --> 00:39:29.690 align:middle line:84%
But yeah, clearly
not all everything

00:39:29.690 --> 00:39:32.190 align:middle line:84%
that is important about writing
is captured in those things.

00:39:32.190 --> 00:39:35.420 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I feel closely
related is language learning.

00:39:35.420 --> 00:39:37.400 align:middle line:84%
You can very easily
assess whether someone

00:39:37.400 --> 00:39:40.460 align:middle line:84%
knows a lot of vocab, or verb
conjugations, or grammar,

00:39:40.460 --> 00:39:42.420 align:middle line:90%
or whatever by via computer.

00:39:42.420 --> 00:39:45.500 align:middle line:84%
But that's not really the
point of language learning.

00:39:45.500 --> 00:39:47.000 align:middle line:84%
Language learning
is-- a lot of it's

00:39:47.000 --> 00:39:50.540 align:middle line:84%
about speaking, and listening,
and writing, and stuff.

00:39:50.540 --> 00:39:54.062 align:middle line:84%
And especially the speaking
and listening part,

00:39:54.062 --> 00:39:56.270 align:middle line:84%
it's probably going to take
a long time for computers

00:39:56.270 --> 00:39:58.340 align:middle line:84%
to do that well,
especially when you

00:39:58.340 --> 00:40:01.940 align:middle line:84%
have variability due to
accents and regional stuff.

00:40:01.940 --> 00:40:05.500 align:middle line:84%
It would be hard to assess
whether or not someone really

00:40:05.500 --> 00:40:08.113 align:middle line:84%
is fluent in a language
by simply interacting

00:40:08.113 --> 00:40:08.780 align:middle line:90%
with a computer.

00:40:08.780 --> 00:40:09.572 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Good.

00:40:09.572 --> 00:40:12.730 align:middle line:84%
And language learning is such an
interesting subject in general

00:40:12.730 --> 00:40:15.850 align:middle line:84%
because it shifts so
much in the middle of it.

00:40:15.850 --> 00:40:18.377 align:middle line:90%
There aren't that many things--

00:40:18.377 --> 00:40:20.710 align:middle line:84%
the kinds of things you do
when you teach social studies

00:40:20.710 --> 00:40:22.570 align:middle line:84%
to third graders is not that
different from the kinds

00:40:22.570 --> 00:40:24.340 align:middle line:84%
of things that you do when you
teach social studies to 12th

00:40:24.340 --> 00:40:24.680 align:middle line:90%
graders.

00:40:24.680 --> 00:40:26.660 align:middle line:84%
It gets more complicated, but
it's along the same lines.

00:40:26.660 --> 00:40:28.300 align:middle line:84%
But the initial
learning of a language

00:40:28.300 --> 00:40:30.320 align:middle line:84%
is more like a
math or a science,

00:40:30.320 --> 00:40:32.080 align:middle line:84%
and then the later
parts of a language

00:40:32.080 --> 00:40:34.780 align:middle line:84%
is more like a humanities or
a social science or something

00:40:34.780 --> 00:40:36.460 align:middle line:90%
like that, which is cool.

00:40:36.460 --> 00:40:41.230 align:middle line:84%
In the book, I write a bunch
about automated pronunciation

00:40:41.230 --> 00:40:47.860 align:middle line:84%
detectors, which, to me, I think
are a great example of advances

00:40:47.860 --> 00:40:49.990 align:middle line:84%
in language learning,
especially when you think

00:40:49.990 --> 00:40:52.870 align:middle line:84%
about the classroom
contexts in which people

00:40:52.870 --> 00:40:54.110 align:middle line:90%
learn to pronounce things.

00:40:54.110 --> 00:40:56.980 align:middle line:84%
So take yourself back to your
middle school Spanish class

00:40:56.980 --> 00:41:00.370 align:middle line:84%
or whatever, and there's
26 kids in the room,

00:41:00.370 --> 00:41:02.010 align:middle line:90%
and there's one teacher.

00:41:02.010 --> 00:41:05.340 align:middle line:84%
And the teacher walks up to you
and goes, "Hola, cómo estás?"

00:41:05.340 --> 00:41:08.900 align:middle line:84%
And then you respond,
and maybe the teacher

00:41:08.900 --> 00:41:11.010 align:middle line:84%
gives you some feedback
about the pronunciation.

00:41:11.010 --> 00:41:12.843 align:middle line:84%
Maybe they're just
whipping around the room.

00:41:12.843 --> 00:41:17.120 align:middle line:84%
But you're getting at best like
1/26 of that teacher's attention

00:41:17.120 --> 00:41:19.620 align:middle line:84%
for that pronunciation
evaluation exercise.

00:41:19.620 --> 00:41:22.070 align:middle line:84%
It's extraordinarily
inefficient.

00:41:22.070 --> 00:41:23.870 align:middle line:84%
I remember this
a lot because I'm

00:41:23.870 --> 00:41:27.500 align:middle line:84%
both bad at language learning
and bad at pronouncing things.

00:41:27.500 --> 00:41:30.080 align:middle line:84%
And so I would say
stuff, and the teacher

00:41:30.080 --> 00:41:33.540 align:middle line:84%
would just nod at me like, OK,
and go on to the next person.

00:41:33.540 --> 00:41:35.420 align:middle line:90%
Clearly, I'm not learning this.

00:41:35.420 --> 00:41:38.420 align:middle line:84%
But I can open up a computer
that has an automated

00:41:38.420 --> 00:41:41.540 align:middle line:84%
pronunciation detection, and
I can just mispronounce "por

00:41:41.540 --> 00:41:43.890 align:middle line:84%
favor" over, and over,
and over, and over again,

00:41:43.890 --> 00:41:45.150 align:middle line:90%
and this thing doesn't care.

00:41:45.150 --> 00:41:47.000 align:middle line:84%
It'll listen to me say it
as many times as it wants.

00:41:47.000 --> 00:41:48.793 align:middle line:84%
As they get better,
they might be able to--

00:41:48.793 --> 00:41:50.210 align:middle line:84%
they can at least
give me examples

00:41:50.210 --> 00:41:51.378 align:middle line:90%
of correct pronunciation.

00:41:51.378 --> 00:41:53.420 align:middle line:84%
They might be able to
fine-tune my pronunciation.

00:41:53.420 --> 00:41:57.710 align:middle line:84%
That seems like a place where
computational assessment might

00:41:57.710 --> 00:42:01.740 align:middle line:84%
really be a whole lot
better than human assessment

00:42:01.740 --> 00:42:03.520 align:middle line:84%
in a domain, that's
pretty important.

00:42:03.520 --> 00:42:04.960 align:middle line:90%
What was your example, Sabrina?

00:42:04.960 --> 00:42:09.270 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I was going to say,
using computers to analyze art.

00:42:09.270 --> 00:42:13.110 align:middle line:84%
It can tell you about the color
compositions, the time period

00:42:13.110 --> 00:42:16.620 align:middle line:84%
that it's from, what
makes it objectively

00:42:16.620 --> 00:42:18.490 align:middle line:90%
appealing to look at.

00:42:18.490 --> 00:42:23.550 align:middle line:84%
But it doesn't necessarily go
into the artist's background

00:42:23.550 --> 00:42:27.850 align:middle line:84%
or the emotional
message behind it

00:42:27.850 --> 00:42:33.670 align:middle line:84%
or why it was created
in the way that it was.

00:42:33.670 --> 00:42:37.780 align:middle line:84%
I think, with art, it's
usually not the surface level,

00:42:37.780 --> 00:42:42.150 align:middle line:84%
which I think that the computer
is really good at hitting.

00:42:42.150 --> 00:42:46.330 align:middle line:84%
But maybe the overall meaning
message is missed entirely.

00:42:46.330 --> 00:42:47.780 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Yeah.

00:42:47.780 --> 00:42:50.820 align:middle line:84%
I mean, there are AP studio art
classes and things like that,

00:42:50.820 --> 00:42:52.380 align:middle line:84%
where you can
submit a portfolio.

00:42:52.380 --> 00:42:56.730 align:middle line:84%
And I've never thought about
it before you brought it up.

00:42:56.730 --> 00:43:00.140 align:middle line:84%
But to my knowledge,
there's no application

00:43:00.140 --> 00:43:03.270 align:middle line:84%
of software in evaluating
a student's art portfolio.

00:43:03.270 --> 00:43:06.800 align:middle line:84%
But it's a kind of fun exercise
to start thinking about, what

00:43:06.800 --> 00:43:09.380 align:middle line:84%
could you assign an AP
studio art student that

00:43:09.380 --> 00:43:14.900 align:middle line:84%
could be graded by a computer
that people would generally

00:43:14.900 --> 00:43:16.640 align:middle line:90%
think is fair?

00:43:16.640 --> 00:43:20.600 align:middle line:84%
Now, clearly, abstract painting
or something like that, probably

00:43:20.600 --> 00:43:23.120 align:middle line:90%
no one will think that's fair.

00:43:23.120 --> 00:43:25.520 align:middle line:84%
Certain kinds of
still life, where

00:43:25.520 --> 00:43:28.730 align:middle line:84%
the task is, reproduce
this bowl of fruit

00:43:28.730 --> 00:43:31.130 align:middle line:84%
as closely as possible
to what the bowl of fruit

00:43:31.130 --> 00:43:33.680 align:middle line:84%
actually looked like,
I would imagine I

00:43:33.680 --> 00:43:36.530 align:middle line:84%
would trust the computer to
do that as well or better

00:43:36.530 --> 00:43:38.683 align:middle line:90%
than a human would.

00:43:38.683 --> 00:43:39.600 align:middle line:90%
But I'm not an artist.

00:43:39.600 --> 00:43:41.990 align:middle line:84%
Maybe there's something about
still lifes I don't understand.

00:43:41.990 --> 00:43:43.550 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: The only
application I can think of--

00:43:43.550 --> 00:43:45.883 align:middle line:84%
and I am very biased because
I do a lot of photography--

00:43:45.883 --> 00:43:48.140 align:middle line:84%
is detecting-- and even
then, it's difficult--

00:43:48.140 --> 00:43:51.140 align:middle line:84%
detecting if something's been
altered in any sort of way

00:43:51.140 --> 00:43:53.670 align:middle line:90%
after the fact, like Photoshop.

00:43:53.670 --> 00:43:57.230 align:middle line:84%
And I know Adobe is working
on their own thing to detect--

00:43:57.230 --> 00:43:58.730 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: What
would be a thing

00:43:58.730 --> 00:44:00.770 align:middle line:84%
that you would want a
novice photographer to be

00:44:00.770 --> 00:44:02.840 align:middle line:84%
able to do pretty well
that a computer could

00:44:02.840 --> 00:44:05.300 align:middle line:84%
tell whether or not
they could do it well?

00:44:05.300 --> 00:44:07.363 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Whether
or not a photo is--

00:44:07.363 --> 00:44:09.530 align:middle line:84%
I think the most fundamental
thing is whether or not

00:44:09.530 --> 00:44:12.170 align:middle line:84%
a photo is exposed
properly without having

00:44:12.170 --> 00:44:14.040 align:middle line:90%
to do any digital alterations.

00:44:14.040 --> 00:44:15.680 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Yeah, yeah.

00:44:15.680 --> 00:44:18.470 align:middle line:84%
And again, if you can
get a computer that

00:44:18.470 --> 00:44:21.770 align:middle line:84%
can do that evaluation,
then you can

00:44:21.770 --> 00:44:24.140 align:middle line:84%
have all of the
photography students

00:44:24.140 --> 00:44:25.940 align:middle line:90%
get more formative feedback.

00:44:25.940 --> 00:44:29.100 align:middle line:84%
Maybe you can get them to have
more high-stakes feedback.

00:44:29.100 --> 00:44:32.300 align:middle line:84%
Maybe we could admit students
into arts colleges based

00:44:32.300 --> 00:44:34.400 align:middle line:84%
on their photography
portfolio, or we can just

00:44:34.400 --> 00:44:36.270 align:middle line:84%
ask them more questions
in their portfolio.

00:44:36.270 --> 00:44:41.870 align:middle line:84%
We can say, humans, go see
whether their intimate portraits

00:44:41.870 --> 00:44:44.252 align:middle line:84%
evoke the character
of the person,

00:44:44.252 --> 00:44:46.460 align:middle line:84%
and don't waste your time
figuring out whether or not

00:44:46.460 --> 00:44:48.860 align:middle line:84%
they can expose photos
correctly because we

00:44:48.860 --> 00:44:50.630 align:middle line:90%
got machines that can do that.

00:44:50.630 --> 00:44:53.420 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, that's a neat example.

00:44:53.420 --> 00:44:55.240 align:middle line:84%
So where were you
most optimistic?

00:44:55.240 --> 00:44:56.740 align:middle line:84%
Where do you feel
like there's going

00:44:56.740 --> 00:44:59.048 align:middle line:84%
to be the most improvement
in the next five years,

00:44:59.048 --> 00:45:01.215 align:middle line:84%
and where are those
improvements going to come from?

00:45:01.215 --> 00:45:04.840 align:middle line:90%


00:45:04.840 --> 00:45:06.590 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I have a
follow-up question.

00:45:06.590 --> 00:45:06.880 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Oh, sure.

00:45:06.880 --> 00:45:07.672 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Is it OK?

00:45:07.672 --> 00:45:08.560 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Yeah.

00:45:08.560 --> 00:45:09.227 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Sorry.

00:45:09.227 --> 00:45:14.020 align:middle line:84%
I was just thinking that if
the photos' exposure level is

00:45:14.020 --> 00:45:16.990 align:middle line:84%
detectable by a
computer, so you begin

00:45:16.990 --> 00:45:21.070 align:middle line:84%
to not have humans evaluate
that on admission tests

00:45:21.070 --> 00:45:25.250 align:middle line:84%
anymore, then soon you'd have
to drop that from the test,

00:45:25.250 --> 00:45:27.970 align:middle line:84%
like that portion of eval--
because students would then

00:45:27.970 --> 00:45:30.700 align:middle line:84%
have access to these kinds
of softwares, as well, which

00:45:30.700 --> 00:45:33.362 align:middle line:84%
means they would never
submit a photo that was not

00:45:33.362 --> 00:45:34.070 align:middle line:90%
exposed properly.

00:45:34.070 --> 00:45:35.920 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: That is
a great description

00:45:35.920 --> 00:45:39.220 align:middle line:84%
of the trap of routine
assessment, that,

00:45:39.220 --> 00:45:42.350 align:middle line:84%
if computers can
detect performance,

00:45:42.350 --> 00:45:45.400 align:middle line:84%
then computers can probably
do that performance.

00:45:45.400 --> 00:45:47.650 align:middle line:84%
If we can build a machine
which tells whether or not

00:45:47.650 --> 00:45:49.670 align:middle line:84%
a photograph that was taken
with the correct exposure,

00:45:49.670 --> 00:45:51.910 align:middle line:84%
then we can just build cameras
that will only take photographs

00:45:51.910 --> 00:45:53.020 align:middle line:84%
with the correct
exposure, and then we

00:45:53.020 --> 00:45:55.730 align:middle line:84%
don't need to teach humans how
to do correct exposures anymore.

00:45:55.730 --> 00:45:59.650 align:middle line:84%
And so that is part of this loop
that educators are constantly

00:45:59.650 --> 00:46:03.140 align:middle line:84%
wrestling with,
constantly thinking about.

00:46:03.140 --> 00:46:06.520 align:middle line:84%
And a thing that makes this
so hard for educators is it's

00:46:06.520 --> 00:46:07.863 align:middle line:90%
really hard to predict--

00:46:07.863 --> 00:46:10.660 align:middle line:90%


00:46:10.660 --> 00:46:13.490 align:middle line:84%
when your old high school
friends are going to call you.

00:46:13.490 --> 00:46:15.580 align:middle line:90%
[LAUGHTER]

00:46:15.580 --> 00:46:18.580 align:middle line:90%
It's really hard--

00:46:18.580 --> 00:46:21.760 align:middle line:90%
I won't say any more.

00:46:21.760 --> 00:46:27.880 align:middle line:84%
It's really hard to
predict in what ways

00:46:27.880 --> 00:46:29.570 align:middle line:84%
these kinds of
disciplines will change.

00:46:29.570 --> 00:46:33.850 align:middle line:84%
Like, how will photography
change over the next five years?

00:46:33.850 --> 00:46:36.698 align:middle line:84%
is a really hard thing
for photography educators

00:46:36.698 --> 00:46:37.490 align:middle line:90%
to guess right now.

00:46:37.490 --> 00:46:40.600 align:middle line:84%
So I've been thinking about
this a lot because I shared this

00:46:40.600 --> 00:46:43.930 align:middle line:84%
paper that we published
with you that was,

00:46:43.930 --> 00:46:46.040 align:middle line:84%
"Generative AI in K-12--
an MIT Perspective."

00:46:46.040 --> 00:46:46.670 align:middle line:90%
I didn't share it with you all.

00:46:46.670 --> 00:46:48.100 align:middle line:90%
I shared it with my students.

00:46:48.100 --> 00:46:50.560 align:middle line:84%
And one of the claims
that we make in there

00:46:50.560 --> 00:46:54.420 align:middle line:84%
is, we can be pretty sure that
computer programming is going

00:46:54.420 --> 00:46:56.160 align:middle line:84%
to change, and that
copilots are going

00:46:56.160 --> 00:46:58.450 align:middle line:84%
to be a really central part
of computer programming.

00:46:58.450 --> 00:47:00.450 align:middle line:84%
And so computer science
teachers should probably

00:47:00.450 --> 00:47:01.320 align:middle line:84%
start thinking about
how they're going

00:47:01.320 --> 00:47:02.487 align:middle line:90%
to change their instruction.

00:47:02.487 --> 00:47:04.498 align:middle line:84%
But I was just at a
conference the other day

00:47:04.498 --> 00:47:06.790 align:middle line:84%
where someone from Cornell
Tech stood up and was like--

00:47:06.790 --> 00:47:08.550 align:middle line:84%
and the whole premise
of the conference

00:47:08.550 --> 00:47:10.768 align:middle line:84%
was, what guidance should
we give to computer science

00:47:10.768 --> 00:47:12.310 align:middle line:84%
teachers to change
their instruction?

00:47:12.310 --> 00:47:14.620 align:middle line:84%
And this woman stood up and
was like, do not do that.

00:47:14.620 --> 00:47:18.600 align:middle line:84%
You have no idea how computer
programming is going to change.

00:47:18.600 --> 00:47:21.300 align:middle line:84%
There's no reason to give
any new advice to teachers

00:47:21.300 --> 00:47:24.510 align:middle line:84%
right now because we have
no idea what that would be.

00:47:24.510 --> 00:47:27.180 align:middle line:84%
And I started thinking
more and more like,

00:47:27.180 --> 00:47:30.390 align:middle line:84%
well, yeah, in part because I've
been hearing from people who

00:47:30.390 --> 00:47:32.460 align:middle line:84%
teach introduction to
computer programming

00:47:32.460 --> 00:47:35.580 align:middle line:84%
that their early efforts
to incorporate copilots

00:47:35.580 --> 00:47:37.920 align:middle line:84%
in instruction were
going pretty poorly,

00:47:37.920 --> 00:47:41.200 align:middle line:84%
that basically, students
were using them,

00:47:41.200 --> 00:47:43.720 align:middle line:84%
and then week 4, week 5 would
roll around, and they're like,

00:47:43.720 --> 00:47:45.130 align:middle line:84%
these students are
totally confused.

00:47:45.130 --> 00:47:47.380 align:middle line:84%
They haven't learned fundamental
things they need to learn.

00:47:47.380 --> 00:47:49.350 align:middle line:84%
And then I talked to
other software engineers

00:47:49.350 --> 00:47:52.580 align:middle line:84%
who were saying things like,
every hour you save programming

00:47:52.580 --> 00:47:56.330 align:middle line:84%
with a copilot will cost you an
hour in quality assurance later,

00:47:56.330 --> 00:47:57.562 align:middle line:90%
that the things--

00:47:57.562 --> 00:47:59.270 align:middle line:84%
you solve little
problems, but you end up

00:47:59.270 --> 00:48:01.550 align:middle line:84%
creating bigger ones, for
some of the reasons that Hal

00:48:01.550 --> 00:48:03.270 align:middle line:90%
has on this screen right here.

00:48:03.270 --> 00:48:08.100 align:middle line:84%
And so then you start saying,
man, maybe we don't know.

00:48:08.100 --> 00:48:11.780 align:middle line:84%
It's pretty hard to think
about how you should change--

00:48:11.780 --> 00:48:15.440 align:middle line:84%
some people might say, why
are we assessing computer

00:48:15.440 --> 00:48:16.663 align:middle line:90%
programming at all anymore?

00:48:16.663 --> 00:48:18.330 align:middle line:84%
The bots are just
going to do it for us.

00:48:18.330 --> 00:48:19.710 align:middle line:84%
And then other people
will be like, yeah,

00:48:19.710 --> 00:48:20.868 align:middle line:90%
I'm not so sure about that.

00:48:20.868 --> 00:48:22.910 align:middle line:84%
We might actually need
people doing this or doing

00:48:22.910 --> 00:48:25.568 align:middle line:90%
parts of this.

00:48:25.568 --> 00:48:27.620 align:middle line:84%
The other thing I
think about a lot

00:48:27.620 --> 00:48:31.440 align:middle line:84%
is that, historically, educators
are not very good at this.

00:48:31.440 --> 00:48:34.403 align:middle line:84%
So if you read, in the 19th
century, the 20th century--

00:48:34.403 --> 00:48:37.070 align:middle line:84%
in the 19th century, there were
a whole bunch of people who were

00:48:37.070 --> 00:48:39.430 align:middle line:84%
fervent believers in
diagramming sentences.

00:48:39.430 --> 00:48:41.180 align:middle line:84%
Did anybody have to
diagram sentences when

00:48:41.180 --> 00:48:42.450 align:middle line:90%
you were in elementary school?

00:48:42.450 --> 00:48:45.708 align:middle line:84%
Subject, predicate,
adverb, all these kind--

00:48:45.708 --> 00:48:47.750 align:middle line:84%
I mean, there are people
who went to their graves

00:48:47.750 --> 00:48:50.300 align:middle line:84%
believing that if we didn't
teach young people what

00:48:50.300 --> 00:48:54.320 align:middle line:84%
the pluperfect was, society
was going to collapse.

00:48:54.320 --> 00:48:57.450 align:middle line:84%
And they were totally wrong,
and we laugh at them today.

00:48:57.450 --> 00:49:00.020 align:middle line:84%
But there's all kinds of stuff
that you and I would sit down

00:49:00.020 --> 00:49:01.280 align:middle line:84%
with a bunch of
curriculum developers

00:49:01.280 --> 00:49:03.488 align:middle line:84%
and be like, man, it's
incredibly important that kids

00:49:03.488 --> 00:49:07.280 align:middle line:84%
in 2024 learn X. And some
fraction of those things

00:49:07.280 --> 00:49:11.040 align:middle line:84%
are absolutely going
to be vital in 2075.

00:49:11.040 --> 00:49:13.370 align:middle line:84%
And some of those things
that we would passionately

00:49:13.370 --> 00:49:15.660 align:middle line:84%
defend right now, people
in 2075 would be like,

00:49:15.660 --> 00:49:18.260 align:middle line:84%
can you believe those
folks 50 years ago thought

00:49:18.260 --> 00:49:20.640 align:middle line:90%
that people needed to learn X?

00:49:20.640 --> 00:49:23.360 align:middle line:84%
Those are like the knuckleheads
who believed in the pluperfect

00:49:23.360 --> 00:49:25.190 align:middle line:84%
in the 19th century or
something like that.

00:49:25.190 --> 00:49:27.215 align:middle line:84%
They are very difficult
things to predict.

00:49:27.215 --> 00:49:29.340 align:middle line:84%
All right, let's talk about
more optimistic things.

00:49:29.340 --> 00:49:30.980 align:middle line:84%
What are other
things, Dana, that--

00:49:30.980 --> 00:49:32.880 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: We had a really
interesting conversation.

00:49:32.880 --> 00:49:34.463 align:middle line:84%
We were saying, if
you go back to what

00:49:34.463 --> 00:49:37.610 align:middle line:84%
you were talking
about earlier in how

00:49:37.610 --> 00:49:40.470 align:middle line:90%
AI can help teachers inspire--

00:49:40.470 --> 00:49:46.040 align:middle line:84%
or AI can inspire teachers
with new homework assignments.

00:49:46.040 --> 00:49:48.850 align:middle line:84%
But then we brought up this
weird, like EdTech Matthew

00:49:48.850 --> 00:49:53.110 align:middle line:90%
effect, where AI only pulls--

00:49:53.110 --> 00:49:55.690 align:middle line:84%
specifically, say,
ChatGPT is only

00:49:55.690 --> 00:49:59.650 align:middle line:84%
pulling from existing
literature on the subject.

00:49:59.650 --> 00:50:03.970 align:middle line:84%
Other than the initial time
that it maybe accidentally puts

00:50:03.970 --> 00:50:06.100 align:middle line:84%
in a random word and
starts going off the walls

00:50:06.100 --> 00:50:08.500 align:middle line:84%
and makes something crazy,
it's really only pulling

00:50:08.500 --> 00:50:10.820 align:middle line:90%
from what is already out there.

00:50:10.820 --> 00:50:14.200 align:middle line:84%
So in the idea of bringing
up the middle class

00:50:14.200 --> 00:50:15.920 align:middle line:84%
and the teachers
with new inspiration,

00:50:15.920 --> 00:50:17.680 align:middle line:84%
you almost have to
pull ahead the elites

00:50:17.680 --> 00:50:19.960 align:middle line:84%
to then produce
the literature that

00:50:19.960 --> 00:50:23.500 align:middle line:84%
is necessary to then
inspire AI with new things

00:50:23.500 --> 00:50:26.820 align:middle line:84%
that will then trickle down
to the middle class for them

00:50:26.820 --> 00:50:27.320 align:middle line:90%
to use.

00:50:27.320 --> 00:50:28.903 align:middle line:84%
So it was almost
this interesting idea

00:50:28.903 --> 00:50:32.380 align:middle line:84%
that AI can be
useful, but it needs

00:50:32.380 --> 00:50:37.000 align:middle line:84%
to have the right
literature to back it up.

00:50:37.000 --> 00:50:39.520 align:middle line:84%
And there's that
interesting dilemma of,

00:50:39.520 --> 00:50:41.360 align:middle line:90%
who is going to create that?

00:50:41.360 --> 00:50:44.860 align:middle line:84%
And how do you inspire people
to push them ahead in that sense

00:50:44.860 --> 00:50:47.270 align:middle line:90%
to help the little guy?

00:50:47.270 --> 00:50:49.330 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: Yeah, what
kind of training data

00:50:49.330 --> 00:50:50.900 align:middle line:84%
gets used is
enormously important.

00:50:50.900 --> 00:50:52.942 align:middle line:84%
We were talking about this
with one of our guests

00:50:52.942 --> 00:50:55.810 align:middle line:90%
from WPI who was saying that--

00:50:55.810 --> 00:50:58.210 align:middle line:84%
maybe whoever was doing that
presentation can explain it

00:50:58.210 --> 00:51:04.130 align:middle line:84%
better than I can-- but that
as LLMs generate more code,

00:51:04.130 --> 00:51:06.790 align:middle line:84%
and that code has
problems with it,

00:51:06.790 --> 00:51:10.450 align:middle line:84%
then future LLMs will pull
in all that buggy code

00:51:10.450 --> 00:51:12.953 align:middle line:90%
and suggest more bad code.

00:51:12.953 --> 00:51:14.120 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Yeah, that was me.

00:51:14.120 --> 00:51:15.340 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: Do you want do
you want to say more about

00:51:15.340 --> 00:51:16.670 align:middle line:90%
what the problem is there?

00:51:16.670 --> 00:51:20.860 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: So I used it to--

00:51:20.860 --> 00:51:25.870 align:middle line:84%
anyway, so it was that
LLMs are trained on Copilot

00:51:25.870 --> 00:51:27.200 align:middle line:90%
on GitHub code.

00:51:27.200 --> 00:51:32.320 align:middle line:84%
So they essentially get all
of the badly written code

00:51:32.320 --> 00:51:33.620 align:middle line:90%
that Copilot generates.

00:51:33.620 --> 00:51:36.700 align:middle line:84%
So when students are
generating that code,

00:51:36.700 --> 00:51:40.640 align:middle line:84%
they are essentially going to
generate the same bad code,

00:51:40.640 --> 00:51:45.990 align:middle line:84%
and they're not going to
fulfill the needs of what

00:51:45.990 --> 00:51:48.510 align:middle line:90%
is required from the question.

00:51:48.510 --> 00:51:54.480 align:middle line:84%
When you give someone an
assignment, the ways to test it

00:51:54.480 --> 00:51:57.850 align:middle line:90%
is to give them some test cases.

00:51:57.850 --> 00:52:02.280 align:middle line:84%
But those test cases essentially
do not solve the entire problem.

00:52:02.280 --> 00:52:08.580 align:middle line:84%
So teaching students how to
build those test cases that

00:52:08.580 --> 00:52:11.850 align:middle line:84%
covers the entire input space
is more important than for them

00:52:11.850 --> 00:52:14.238 align:middle line:90%
to be able to write code.

00:52:14.238 --> 00:52:15.030 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Good.

00:52:15.030 --> 00:52:15.530 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:52:15.530 --> 00:52:17.100 align:middle line:90%
And so that was--

00:52:17.100 --> 00:52:19.420 align:middle line:84%
I mean, who knows if what
you just said is true,

00:52:19.420 --> 00:52:21.970 align:middle line:84%
but that's a great
claim about the future.

00:52:21.970 --> 00:52:23.745 align:middle line:84%
Building certain
kind of test cases

00:52:23.745 --> 00:52:26.370 align:middle line:84%
is going to be more important to
the future of computer science

00:52:26.370 --> 00:52:29.850 align:middle line:84%
than being able to write
certain lines of codes

00:52:29.850 --> 00:52:32.920 align:middle line:84%
or knowing other programming
principles or things like that.

00:52:32.920 --> 00:52:34.410 align:middle line:84%
This is exactly
what educators are

00:52:34.410 --> 00:52:36.210 align:middle line:84%
wrestling with
every time there's

00:52:36.210 --> 00:52:39.180 align:middle line:90%
some new technological change.

00:52:39.180 --> 00:52:41.148 align:middle line:84%
Do we still teach kids
spelling in an age

00:52:41.148 --> 00:52:42.190 align:middle line:90%
when we have spell check?

00:52:42.190 --> 00:52:44.037 align:middle line:84%
Do we still teach kids
computation in an age

00:52:44.037 --> 00:52:45.120 align:middle line:90%
where there are computers?

00:52:45.120 --> 00:52:48.080 align:middle line:84%
These kinds of things come
up over and over again.

00:52:48.080 --> 00:52:49.550 align:middle line:84%
What were you going
to say, Avril?

00:52:49.550 --> 00:52:51.230 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Something
that maybe could

00:52:51.230 --> 00:52:55.040 align:middle line:84%
be an optimistic outcome
is that software is

00:52:55.040 --> 00:52:58.020 align:middle line:90%
a language-oriented discipline.

00:52:58.020 --> 00:53:01.100 align:middle line:84%
And that's kind of why ChatGPT
and language-based models

00:53:01.100 --> 00:53:02.155 align:middle line:90%
are so good at doing it.

00:53:02.155 --> 00:53:03.530 align:middle line:84%
But there are
things that aren't.

00:53:03.530 --> 00:53:05.610 align:middle line:84%
Analog circuit design
is very visual.

00:53:05.610 --> 00:53:08.580 align:middle line:84%
And right now, signal
processing, like training,

00:53:08.580 --> 00:53:11.690 align:middle line:84%
machine learning models
with images and things,

00:53:11.690 --> 00:53:12.810 align:middle line:90%
is a lot more difficult.

00:53:12.810 --> 00:53:16.190 align:middle line:84%
So for at least a few
decades, hopefully,

00:53:16.190 --> 00:53:17.780 align:middle line:84%
analog circuit design
and other things

00:53:17.780 --> 00:53:20.970 align:middle line:84%
that are related to hardware
will be good skills,

00:53:20.970 --> 00:53:26.670 align:middle line:84%
probably, to teach kids and to
build decent fields to work in.

00:53:26.670 --> 00:53:29.600 align:middle line:84%
Also, even though there are
copilot things for things

00:53:29.600 --> 00:53:35.060 align:middle line:84%
like cadence, such as layout for
semiconductor chips or whatever,

00:53:35.060 --> 00:53:36.330 align:middle line:90%
I don't think they're good.

00:53:36.330 --> 00:53:37.663 align:middle line:90%
I also don't know how they work.

00:53:37.663 --> 00:53:41.540 align:middle line:84%
So there's a more
strong emphasis

00:53:41.540 --> 00:53:44.090 align:middle line:84%
on teaching kids
the basics of how

00:53:44.090 --> 00:53:47.660 align:middle line:84%
the things that
they are, I guess,

00:53:47.660 --> 00:53:50.000 align:middle line:84%
catering to with
ChatGPT actually work.

00:53:50.000 --> 00:53:51.620 align:middle line:84%
What I'm trying to
say is that I hope

00:53:51.620 --> 00:53:57.410 align:middle line:84%
that people have this shift
towards understanding how things

00:53:57.410 --> 00:53:59.640 align:middle line:84%
work from the bottom
instead of the top,

00:53:59.640 --> 00:54:01.970 align:middle line:84%
which is good because
then you can build things

00:54:01.970 --> 00:54:03.357 align:middle line:90%
from the ground up.

00:54:03.357 --> 00:54:04.440 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Well, great.

00:54:04.440 --> 00:54:05.570 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, I like that.

00:54:05.570 --> 00:54:06.480 align:middle line:84%
What were you going
to say, Aaron?

00:54:06.480 --> 00:54:07.438 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: I don't know.

00:54:07.438 --> 00:54:10.440 align:middle line:84%
I have a disagreement with
the analog circuit design,

00:54:10.440 --> 00:54:12.770 align:middle line:84%
but that's just purely
because anything--

00:54:12.770 --> 00:54:15.330 align:middle line:84%
because it's still a visual
software that's doing it,

00:54:15.330 --> 00:54:19.430 align:middle line:84%
so there is some translation
that could be considered.

00:54:19.430 --> 00:54:22.040 align:middle line:84%
I do know a couple of analog
circuit design softwares

00:54:22.040 --> 00:54:23.425 align:middle line:90%
that just translate down to XML.

00:54:23.425 --> 00:54:25.020 align:middle line:84%
And you could just
stick that XML,

00:54:25.020 --> 00:54:27.570 align:middle line:84%
which is a structured,
object-oriented language,

00:54:27.570 --> 00:54:31.345 align:middle line:84%
into ChatGPT, get new XML back,
validate that it's correct,

00:54:31.345 --> 00:54:32.720 align:middle line:84%
see it, and then
it will probably

00:54:32.720 --> 00:54:35.450 align:middle line:84%
produce a correct
circuit in some capacity.

00:54:35.450 --> 00:54:36.410 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: But can you--

00:54:36.410 --> 00:54:39.830 align:middle line:84%
I mean, where are you starting
when you think about designing

00:54:39.830 --> 00:54:42.670 align:middle line:90%
an amplifier or something?

00:54:42.670 --> 00:54:44.402 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: It's starting from--

00:54:44.402 --> 00:54:46.360 align:middle line:84%
well, it's the same thing
when you started now.

00:54:46.360 --> 00:54:48.200 align:middle line:84%
Nobody had code when
they first started.

00:54:48.200 --> 00:54:50.170 align:middle line:84%
You would have whatever
data set of people

00:54:50.170 --> 00:54:53.390 align:middle line:84%
has all their amplifier
files that are all lined up,

00:54:53.390 --> 00:54:55.580 align:middle line:90%
whatever it is.

00:54:55.580 --> 00:54:59.830 align:middle line:84%
Then you would decide, OK, let's
get the textual representation

00:54:59.830 --> 00:55:02.560 align:middle line:84%
of this in some syntactical
language, which XML was just

00:55:02.560 --> 00:55:04.060 align:middle line:84%
the one that came
to my head, but it

00:55:04.060 --> 00:55:06.850 align:middle line:84%
could be anything that can
represent the image in text

00:55:06.850 --> 00:55:09.340 align:middle line:84%
form, since that's all the
software is doing, since it's

00:55:09.340 --> 00:55:11.590 align:middle line:84%
just literally inputs and
outputs of where you connect

00:55:11.590 --> 00:55:13.280 align:middle line:84%
wire A to wire B
or wherever it is,

00:55:13.280 --> 00:55:15.020 align:middle line:90%
and that can usually be limited.

00:55:15.020 --> 00:55:17.960 align:middle line:84%
And then you take that
text, train the LLM on it,

00:55:17.960 --> 00:55:20.090 align:middle line:84%
you run it through
it, you get some out.

00:55:20.090 --> 00:55:22.450 align:middle line:84%
You then say, OK,
generate me an amplifier,

00:55:22.450 --> 00:55:24.728 align:middle line:84%
and it will generate you
an amplifier with that XML.

00:55:24.728 --> 00:55:27.020 align:middle line:84%
You put it back into the
visual software, and now look,

00:55:27.020 --> 00:55:28.178 align:middle line:90%
you now have an amplifier.

00:55:28.178 --> 00:55:28.720 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: OK.

00:55:28.720 --> 00:55:31.690 align:middle line:84%
My new hope is that all of
this becomes proprietary,

00:55:31.690 --> 00:55:33.694 align:middle line:90%
and they don't let--

00:55:33.694 --> 00:55:35.980 align:middle line:90%
[LAUGHTER]

00:55:35.980 --> 00:55:37.810 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: And
I think, to me,

00:55:37.810 --> 00:55:39.410 align:middle line:84%
from an educator's
point of view,

00:55:39.410 --> 00:55:42.220 align:middle line:84%
what's so challenging about
these kinds of questions

00:55:42.220 --> 00:55:43.938 align:middle line:84%
is I know nothing
about your domain,

00:55:43.938 --> 00:55:45.730 align:middle line:84%
and so both of the
cases that you just made

00:55:45.730 --> 00:55:49.720 align:middle line:84%
seem totally plausible to me,
and that it's really hard--

00:55:49.720 --> 00:55:53.980 align:middle line:84%
people seem to be really bad at
predicting the kinds of things

00:55:53.980 --> 00:55:56.600 align:middle line:84%
that AI are going to be
useful for in the future.

00:55:56.600 --> 00:55:59.260 align:middle line:84%
Which of these problems are
solvable and which are they not?

00:55:59.260 --> 00:56:00.968 align:middle line:84%
So you could imagine
somewhere out there,

00:56:00.968 --> 00:56:07.480 align:middle line:84%
there are people who run the
ABET engineering curriculum

00:56:07.480 --> 00:56:09.280 align:middle line:84%
certification kinds
of things, or people

00:56:09.280 --> 00:56:10.993 align:middle line:84%
who are department
heads, or people

00:56:10.993 --> 00:56:12.910 align:middle line:84%
who are writing textbooks
or things like that.

00:56:12.910 --> 00:56:15.260 align:middle line:84%
Which of this stuff
do we want to keep?

00:56:15.260 --> 00:56:16.890 align:middle line:84%
Because we'll still
need human beings

00:56:16.890 --> 00:56:18.640 align:middle line:84%
to build-- for the
three categories, which

00:56:18.640 --> 00:56:21.230 align:middle line:84%
are the stuff is like,
nah, in the future,

00:56:21.230 --> 00:56:23.140 align:middle line:90%
just AI will build this.

00:56:23.140 --> 00:56:25.353 align:middle line:84%
You'll tell some machine
learning system, AI system

00:56:25.353 --> 00:56:27.770 align:middle line:84%
the kind of circuit that you
want, and it'll print it out.

00:56:27.770 --> 00:56:28.600 align:middle line:84%
And so you don't really
know that you need

00:56:28.600 --> 00:56:29.725 align:middle line:90%
to know that much about it.

00:56:29.725 --> 00:56:33.377 align:middle line:84%
B, yeah, an AI will probably
print out most of these things,

00:56:33.377 --> 00:56:34.960 align:middle line:84%
but it's still useful
for humans to be

00:56:34.960 --> 00:56:37.893 align:middle line:84%
able to understand this
thing because that gives you

00:56:37.893 --> 00:56:39.310 align:middle line:84%
some kind of insight
about how you

00:56:39.310 --> 00:56:41.560 align:middle line:84%
would build other technology,
other things like that.

00:56:41.560 --> 00:56:43.060 align:middle line:84%
Or, no, there's no
way computers are

00:56:43.060 --> 00:56:44.352 align:middle line:90%
going to be good at this stuff.

00:56:44.352 --> 00:56:46.480 align:middle line:84%
We still need human beings
of primarily to do it.

00:56:46.480 --> 00:56:49.060 align:middle line:84%
Those are at least three
categories of things,

00:56:49.060 --> 00:56:51.280 align:middle line:84%
and it's really hard
to predict which things

00:56:51.280 --> 00:56:52.490 align:middle line:90%
move into which categories.

00:56:52.490 --> 00:56:55.900 align:middle line:84%
And it's very difficult to get
education systems to be good

00:56:55.900 --> 00:56:59.080 align:middle line:84%
at doing new kinds of things,
which is why most of the schools

00:56:59.080 --> 00:57:02.440 align:middle line:84%
that you went to had very little
computer science education

00:57:02.440 --> 00:57:05.200 align:middle line:84%
and stuff like that because,
in the 19th century when we

00:57:05.200 --> 00:57:07.430 align:middle line:84%
designed high schools, there
wasn't computer science,

00:57:07.430 --> 00:57:09.735 align:middle line:84%
and so we didn't put
it in the curriculum.

00:57:09.735 --> 00:57:11.860 align:middle line:84%
And we're a lot better at
doing the kinds of things

00:57:11.860 --> 00:57:13.902 align:middle line:84%
we've done before than
doing new kinds of things.

00:57:13.902 --> 00:57:16.023 align:middle line:90%


00:57:16.023 --> 00:57:16.690 align:middle line:90%
All right, good.

00:57:16.690 --> 00:57:19.460 align:middle line:84%
Were there other observations
or thoughts that people had?

00:57:19.460 --> 00:57:22.990 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I think
technology can bring

00:57:22.990 --> 00:57:24.710 align:middle line:84%
about a change in
the legal system.

00:57:24.710 --> 00:57:28.360 align:middle line:90%
So a lot of people--

00:57:28.360 --> 00:57:31.720 align:middle line:84%
lawyers spend a lot of time
reading stuff and building

00:57:31.720 --> 00:57:34.790 align:middle line:90%
upon that for a specific case.

00:57:34.790 --> 00:57:38.070 align:middle line:84%
And that's what
determines their career.

00:57:38.070 --> 00:57:41.490 align:middle line:84%
How big you get depends
on how much you've read,

00:57:41.490 --> 00:57:42.960 align:middle line:90%
how much you have experienced.

00:57:42.960 --> 00:57:47.300 align:middle line:84%
So if all this knowledge is
readily available to them

00:57:47.300 --> 00:57:50.570 align:middle line:84%
for a specific test
case, then probably it's

00:57:50.570 --> 00:57:53.078 align:middle line:84%
going to make their
lives much more easier.

00:57:53.078 --> 00:57:53.870 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Yeah.

00:57:53.870 --> 00:57:58.220 align:middle line:84%
I think some of framework that
Levy and Murnane offer also

00:57:58.220 --> 00:58:00.905 align:middle line:84%
help us think about, what
kinds of things will lawyers

00:58:00.905 --> 00:58:02.280 align:middle line:84%
need to be good
at in the future?

00:58:02.280 --> 00:58:04.670 align:middle line:84%
To me, it strikes
me as very unlikely

00:58:04.670 --> 00:58:09.230 align:middle line:84%
that computers will have much
impact on the part of trial

00:58:09.230 --> 00:58:13.910 align:middle line:84%
lawyer work, which is
convincing juries of things.

00:58:13.910 --> 00:58:16.250 align:middle line:84%
The people who actually
get in front of juries

00:58:16.250 --> 00:58:19.730 align:middle line:84%
and try cases and things
like that, it's very unlikely

00:58:19.730 --> 00:58:21.830 align:middle line:84%
that we'll have AI
machines that generate

00:58:21.830 --> 00:58:24.290 align:middle line:84%
scripts for them or things
like that because that kind

00:58:24.290 --> 00:58:26.390 align:middle line:84%
of persuasive,
complex communication

00:58:26.390 --> 00:58:28.550 align:middle line:84%
seems to be the domain
in which human beings are

00:58:28.550 --> 00:58:30.260 align:middle line:90%
most specialized at.

00:58:30.260 --> 00:58:34.140 align:middle line:84%
We probably-- expert thinking
seems to be the domain

00:58:34.140 --> 00:58:34.640 align:middle line:90%
in which--

00:58:34.640 --> 00:58:39.170 align:middle line:90%


00:58:39.170 --> 00:58:41.270 align:middle line:84%
are people going to
write AI software that

00:58:41.270 --> 00:58:43.250 align:middle line:90%
detects tax loopholes?

00:58:43.250 --> 00:58:44.360 align:middle line:90%
Absolutely.

00:58:44.360 --> 00:58:48.560 align:middle line:84%
Somebody is going to come up
with all kinds of new ways

00:58:48.560 --> 00:58:50.960 align:middle line:84%
that are much faster than
humans at finding ways

00:58:50.960 --> 00:58:52.850 align:middle line:90%
to defeat the tax code.

00:58:52.850 --> 00:58:58.190 align:middle line:84%
But there probably still
will be human beings who

00:58:58.190 --> 00:59:00.470 align:middle line:84%
are much better at
machines, at recognizing,

00:59:00.470 --> 00:59:06.030 align:middle line:84%
oh, well, this part might be
slightly strictly illegal,

00:59:06.030 --> 00:59:11.040 align:middle line:84%
but because of the way-- or
its legality is not tested,

00:59:11.040 --> 00:59:13.220 align:middle line:84%
but I happen to know,
because of everything that I

00:59:13.220 --> 00:59:15.600 align:middle line:84%
know about the
functioning of society,

00:59:15.600 --> 00:59:17.900 align:middle line:84%
that this is a particularly
weak point in the tax code

00:59:17.900 --> 00:59:22.670 align:middle line:84%
and would be a good
place to shelter profits

00:59:22.670 --> 00:59:24.470 align:middle line:90%
and those kinds of things.

00:59:24.470 --> 00:59:26.860 align:middle line:84%
And then there does seem
to be some kind of--

00:59:26.860 --> 00:59:31.310 align:middle line:84%
the more a legal task veers
into the routine category,

00:59:31.310 --> 00:59:34.888 align:middle line:84%
the more it's, hey,
we just need to know

00:59:34.888 --> 00:59:37.180 align:middle line:84%
how many times in these
documents these things are said

00:59:37.180 --> 00:59:38.330 align:middle line:90%
or something like that.

00:59:38.330 --> 00:59:41.540 align:middle line:84%
But, I mean, to me, it's useful
to recognize that, I mean,

00:59:41.540 --> 00:59:47.230 align:middle line:84%
people were stressed
about that in 2017

00:59:47.230 --> 00:59:49.010 align:middle line:84%
with the machine
learning technology.

00:59:49.010 --> 00:59:51.590 align:middle line:84%
So there are still lawyers
in the United States.

00:59:51.590 --> 00:59:52.930 align:middle line:90%
There are still law schools.

00:59:52.930 --> 00:59:55.210 align:middle line:84%
Our legal system
has not profoundly

00:59:55.210 --> 00:59:58.840 align:middle line:84%
changed from what
machine learning offered.

00:59:58.840 --> 01:00:02.530 align:middle line:84%
In fact, I think legal education
has probably barely changed

01:00:02.530 --> 01:00:04.870 align:middle line:84%
at all on the basis of these
things, except maybe you

01:00:04.870 --> 01:00:05.540 align:middle line:90%
could take an--

01:00:05.540 --> 01:00:06.410 align:middle line:90%
I don't know this for sure.

01:00:06.410 --> 01:00:07.370 align:middle line:90%
I have no expertise in this.

01:00:07.370 --> 01:00:08.830 align:middle line:84%
But I assume you
could, take an elective

01:00:08.830 --> 01:00:11.205 align:middle line:84%
in computers in the law or
something like that or machine

01:00:11.205 --> 01:00:14.260 align:middle line:84%
learning and the law now you
wouldn't be able to do before.

01:00:14.260 --> 01:00:16.030 align:middle line:90%
Do you guys have thoughts?

01:00:16.030 --> 01:00:18.050 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

01:00:18.050 --> 01:00:20.830 align:middle line:84%
In terms of when you initially
say back about lawyers

01:00:20.830 --> 01:00:23.060 align:middle line:84%
and not being able
to generate scripts,

01:00:23.060 --> 01:00:25.960 align:middle line:90%
yeah, I would very much agree.

01:00:25.960 --> 01:00:28.960 align:middle line:84%
There's actually a case
where I think two lawyers got

01:00:28.960 --> 01:00:29.865 align:middle line:90%
heavily sanctioned--

01:00:29.865 --> 01:00:31.240 align:middle line:84%
I think it was
with the airport--

01:00:31.240 --> 01:00:32.615 align:middle line:84%
where they pretty
much just tried

01:00:32.615 --> 01:00:35.260 align:middle line:84%
to generate an entire script,
purely through ChatGPT.

01:00:35.260 --> 01:00:36.920 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: I think
it was a filing,

01:00:36.920 --> 01:00:40.840 align:middle line:84%
and I think the filing included
fake citations and stuff

01:00:40.840 --> 01:00:43.160 align:middle line:84%
like that, which is
really not the best.

01:00:43.160 --> 01:00:45.160 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Yeah, and you
could definitely see it,

01:00:45.160 --> 01:00:46.910 align:middle line:84%
where basically,
if you give it--

01:00:46.910 --> 01:00:50.560 align:middle line:84%
you could basically make a much
better search database using--

01:00:50.560 --> 01:00:53.620 align:middle line:84%
if you happen to stick in
all these thousands of cases

01:00:53.620 --> 01:00:57.370 align:middle line:84%
where all these lawyers are
pretty much having decisions

01:00:57.370 --> 01:01:02.110 align:middle line:84%
that change, and maybe one
that benefits your stance

01:01:02.110 --> 01:01:05.600 align:middle line:84%
could have been ruled on
earlier on in that year.

01:01:05.600 --> 01:01:07.430 align:middle line:84%
Maybe it's for the
specific county,

01:01:07.430 --> 01:01:09.400 align:middle line:90%
there is something different.

01:01:09.400 --> 01:01:11.142 align:middle line:84%
I definitely see
that in the future--

01:01:11.142 --> 01:01:13.100 align:middle line:84%
I don't know-- even if
within that five years--

01:01:13.100 --> 01:01:15.970 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: Yeah,
being able to do

01:01:15.970 --> 01:01:18.980 align:middle line:84%
scans of precedents for a
case or something like that.

01:01:18.980 --> 01:01:19.690 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

01:01:19.690 --> 01:01:21.190 align:middle line:84%
JUSTIN REICH: Maybe
one other domain

01:01:21.190 --> 01:01:23.770 align:middle line:84%
we should talk about
before we head out is just,

01:01:23.770 --> 01:01:25.990 align:middle line:84%
one of the biggest
places in which we

01:01:25.990 --> 01:01:28.900 align:middle line:84%
will see pushes in the K-12
education system with new tools

01:01:28.900 --> 01:01:33.708 align:middle line:84%
will be in formative
feedback for writing.

01:01:33.708 --> 01:01:35.250 align:middle line:84%
There are many, many
people right now

01:01:35.250 --> 01:01:37.050 align:middle line:84%
who are building
systems so that you

01:01:37.050 --> 01:01:39.480 align:middle line:84%
assign students to write an
essay, they turn in the essay

01:01:39.480 --> 01:01:40.560 align:middle line:90%
to you--

01:01:40.560 --> 01:01:42.570 align:middle line:84%
or before they turn
in the essay to you,

01:01:42.570 --> 01:01:44.910 align:middle line:84%
you offer a bunch of
formative feedback.

01:01:44.910 --> 01:01:48.730 align:middle line:84%
And I'm very interested in the
development of these systems.

01:01:48.730 --> 01:01:53.830 align:middle line:84%
I was talking with someone
who's building one at UC Irvine,

01:01:53.830 --> 01:01:56.490 align:middle line:84%
and I guess I was reading
about some others, too.

01:01:56.490 --> 01:01:59.220 align:middle line:84%
It struck me that one of the
things that these systems have

01:01:59.220 --> 01:02:04.050 align:middle line:84%
the capacity of doing is
saying something like, here are

01:02:04.050 --> 01:02:06.840 align:middle line:84%
10 or 15 or 100
things that I want

01:02:06.840 --> 01:02:10.230 align:middle line:90%
you to check for every essay.

01:02:10.230 --> 01:02:14.490 align:middle line:84%
Does the paragraph start
with a topic sentence?

01:02:14.490 --> 01:02:16.230 align:middle line:84%
Is there a transition
sentence between?

01:02:16.230 --> 01:02:18.250 align:middle line:84%
Does the paragraph seem
like it has evidence?

01:02:18.250 --> 01:02:20.790 align:middle line:84%
Does the introduction
appear to have a thesis?

01:02:20.790 --> 01:02:24.030 align:middle line:84%
Does the conclusion tie-- you
can think about all the things

01:02:24.030 --> 01:02:26.130 align:middle line:84%
that you've ever
been taught to write.

01:02:26.130 --> 01:02:28.590 align:middle line:84%
A thing that I'm
really interested in

01:02:28.590 --> 01:02:30.287 align:middle line:90%
is that, my hunch is--

01:02:30.287 --> 01:02:32.870 align:middle line:84%
I mean, I thought about these a
lot because a lot of what I do

01:02:32.870 --> 01:02:35.510 align:middle line:84%
is grade writing, give
feedback on writing--

01:02:35.510 --> 01:02:39.360 align:middle line:84%
is that some of this seems
intuitively helpful to me.

01:02:39.360 --> 01:02:42.690 align:middle line:84%
Oh, it would be great if I
could just tell students, hey,

01:02:42.690 --> 01:02:44.750 align:middle line:84%
before you turn in
your assignment,

01:02:44.750 --> 01:02:47.198 align:middle line:84%
go run it through this
thing and make sure

01:02:47.198 --> 01:02:48.740 align:middle line:84%
that the beginning
of every paragraph

01:02:48.740 --> 01:02:50.570 align:middle line:84%
has a topic sentence,
even better than a topic

01:02:50.570 --> 01:02:51.810 align:middle line:84%
sentence, some kind
of mini thesis,

01:02:51.810 --> 01:02:54.140 align:middle line:84%
something that presents the
argument of the paragraph.

01:02:54.140 --> 01:02:55.730 align:middle line:84%
But I was thinking,
too, probably

01:02:55.730 --> 01:03:00.290 align:middle line:84%
the most important thing that
I do when I give feedback

01:03:00.290 --> 01:03:04.640 align:middle line:84%
to someone is figure
out, of the dozens

01:03:04.640 --> 01:03:06.830 align:middle line:84%
of things I could
comment on, which one

01:03:06.830 --> 01:03:08.028 align:middle line:90%
I will choose to comment on.

01:03:08.028 --> 01:03:10.070 align:middle line:84%
One of the things I've
learned about as a writing

01:03:10.070 --> 01:03:12.380 align:middle line:84%
teacher over the years
is, it is really not

01:03:12.380 --> 01:03:15.442 align:middle line:84%
helpful to tell
someone everything they

01:03:15.442 --> 01:03:17.900 align:middle line:84%
can prove in their essay because
that's just too much stuff

01:03:17.900 --> 01:03:18.420 align:middle line:90%
to work on.

01:03:18.420 --> 01:03:19.670 align:middle line:90%
I mean, you guys know that I--

01:03:19.670 --> 01:03:22.520 align:middle line:84%
I mean, I used to have people
turn in printed essays,

01:03:22.520 --> 01:03:25.250 align:middle line:84%
and I used to make marks on
them and things like that.

01:03:25.250 --> 01:03:27.140 align:middle line:84%
But I found over the
years, really what

01:03:27.140 --> 01:03:30.080 align:middle line:84%
I want to do is be like, these
are the two things that you

01:03:30.080 --> 01:03:31.220 align:middle line:90%
want to work on.

01:03:31.220 --> 01:03:34.650 align:middle line:84%
And how do I decide what
those two things are?

01:03:34.650 --> 01:03:38.030 align:middle line:84%
I have in my head a model
of people's developmental

01:03:38.030 --> 01:03:38.730 align:middle line:90%
trajectory.

01:03:38.730 --> 01:03:43.052 align:middle line:84%
And some of that model is
based on all MIT students ever.

01:03:43.052 --> 01:03:44.760 align:middle line:84%
When I see an essay
that looks like this,

01:03:44.760 --> 01:03:46.310 align:middle line:84%
I'm pretty sure that the next
thing they should work on

01:03:46.310 --> 01:03:46.770 align:middle line:90%
is blank.

01:03:46.770 --> 01:03:48.978 align:middle line:84%
And then as soon as I've
gotten two pieces of writing

01:03:48.978 --> 01:03:50.730 align:middle line:84%
from someone, then
I can be like, well,

01:03:50.730 --> 01:03:52.470 align:middle line:84%
the thing you were working
on last time was this.

01:03:52.470 --> 01:03:53.730 align:middle line:90%
Why don't you work on it more?

01:03:53.730 --> 01:03:55.522 align:middle line:84%
Here's the next thing
after that to work on

01:03:55.522 --> 01:03:56.690 align:middle line:90%
or something like that.

01:03:56.690 --> 01:03:59.130 align:middle line:84%
That seems like the
kind of discernment--

01:03:59.130 --> 01:04:01.640 align:middle line:84%
it seems way, way
easier to me to program

01:04:01.640 --> 01:04:06.170 align:middle line:84%
a machine that can find
every kind of problem

01:04:06.170 --> 01:04:08.438 align:middle line:84%
than it is to program a
machine that can figure out,

01:04:08.438 --> 01:04:10.730 align:middle line:84%
what are the next two problems
you should be working on

01:04:10.730 --> 01:04:13.360 align:middle line:84%
as a learner based
on who you are,

01:04:13.360 --> 01:04:15.110 align:middle line:84%
or where I think you're
heading, or what's

01:04:15.110 --> 01:04:17.160 align:middle line:84%
important in my class, or
other things like that.

01:04:17.160 --> 01:04:18.618 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I feel
like that opens up

01:04:18.618 --> 01:04:22.250 align:middle line:84%
the idea of breaking out of
the trap of routine assessment.

01:04:22.250 --> 01:04:24.900 align:middle line:84%
If a computer can do
those type of things,

01:04:24.900 --> 01:04:27.200 align:middle line:84%
then maybe teachers
can now focus more on,

01:04:27.200 --> 01:04:28.820 align:middle line:90%
what does revision look like?

01:04:28.820 --> 01:04:32.085 align:middle line:84%
So a student goes
through an essay

01:04:32.085 --> 01:04:34.460 align:middle line:84%
or writes an essay and then
puts it through this machine,

01:04:34.460 --> 01:04:38.680 align:middle line:84%
and the machine tells you, oh,
in these introduction sentences,

01:04:38.680 --> 01:04:40.970 align:middle line:84%
they could be stronger
in X, Y, and Z ways.

01:04:40.970 --> 01:04:43.300 align:middle line:84%
And how do you develop those
critical thinking skills

01:04:43.300 --> 01:04:48.700 align:middle line:84%
to take something and feedback
and perform it-- or change it

01:04:48.700 --> 01:04:49.310 align:middle line:90%
in some way?

01:04:49.310 --> 01:04:50.590 align:middle line:84%
Like you're always
saying, anything

01:04:50.590 --> 01:04:52.632 align:middle line:84%
that you've published was
never your first draft,

01:04:52.632 --> 01:04:55.270 align:middle line:84%
or you said, on day
one, of your good skills

01:04:55.270 --> 01:04:58.130 align:middle line:84%
was being able to just
get down a first draft.

01:04:58.130 --> 01:04:59.810 align:middle line:90%
ChatGPT can do the same for you.

01:04:59.810 --> 01:05:02.440 align:middle line:84%
But how do we teach
people in a world

01:05:02.440 --> 01:05:04.310 align:middle line:84%
where AI can give
you that baseline?

01:05:04.310 --> 01:05:10.610 align:middle line:84%
How do we elevate it to a status
that is acceptable for a human?

01:05:10.610 --> 01:05:13.190 align:middle line:84%
I feel like AI can
give us a baseline,

01:05:13.190 --> 01:05:15.880 align:middle line:84%
but humans take that
imagination and creativity

01:05:15.880 --> 01:05:16.880 align:middle line:90%
to that next step.

01:05:16.880 --> 01:05:19.842 align:middle line:84%
And so you can take the
idea of, here's an essay.

01:05:19.842 --> 01:05:21.800 align:middle line:84%
How would you change it
to make it even better?

01:05:21.800 --> 01:05:22.592 align:middle line:90%
JUSTIN REICH: Yeah.

01:05:22.592 --> 01:05:25.370 align:middle line:84%
For these writing
software developers,

01:05:25.370 --> 01:05:27.910 align:middle line:84%
I mean, a big-- well, when I
talk to them, I think about,

01:05:27.910 --> 01:05:30.770 align:middle line:84%
what is the whole system
that you're constructing?

01:05:30.770 --> 01:05:34.820 align:middle line:84%
Not every capacity that you
build will end up being useful,

01:05:34.820 --> 01:05:37.120 align:middle line:84%
not just because it doesn't
work, but because using it

01:05:37.120 --> 01:05:37.953 align:middle line:90%
is not that helpful.

01:05:37.953 --> 01:05:40.450 align:middle line:84%
You start thinking
things like, all right,

01:05:40.450 --> 01:05:44.260 align:middle line:84%
what if there were these
formative feedback systems where

01:05:44.260 --> 01:05:51.492 align:middle line:84%
I as the instructor can say, OK,
start this package of machines

01:05:51.492 --> 01:05:52.450 align:middle line:90%
or something like that?

01:05:52.450 --> 01:05:56.050 align:middle line:84%
Or I select in
advance that I want

01:05:56.050 --> 01:06:00.040 align:middle line:84%
the machine to automatically
give you formative feedback

01:06:00.040 --> 01:06:00.770 align:middle line:90%
on these things.

01:06:00.770 --> 01:06:02.180 align:middle line:84%
And then on this
other class of things,

01:06:02.180 --> 01:06:04.030 align:middle line:84%
I want you to show me the
feedback that you would give

01:06:04.030 --> 01:06:06.320 align:middle line:84%
to a student, but I will
pick from those things,

01:06:06.320 --> 01:06:08.590 align:middle line:84%
and then there'll be some
way for me to add to it.

01:06:08.590 --> 01:06:10.990 align:middle line:84%
Some of those
human-machine combinations

01:06:10.990 --> 01:06:12.460 align:middle line:90%
seem pretty exciting to me.

01:06:12.460 --> 01:06:13.390 align:middle line:90%
Good.

01:06:13.390 --> 01:06:17.790 align:middle line:84%
Well, why don't we
wrap there for the day?

01:06:17.790 --> 01:06:26.000 align:middle line:90%